U({id:'maths',title:'Maths for operators',icon:'√x',color:'#7F77DD',desc:'Powers of ten, exponentials, logs, rates of change and counting statistics.',lessons:[
L('m1','Powers of ten and units',8,[
T(`<h3>Tiny numbers and huge numbers</h3><p>A reactor deals with numbers from tiny to enormous. A neutron in a reactor core lives for about <b>0.00002 seconds</b>. The core makes about <b>3,000,000,000 watts</b> of heat.</p><p>Scientific notation keeps these readable: 2 × 10<sup>−5</sup> s and 3 × 10<sup>9</sup> W.</p>`),
Q(`Which of these is 3 × 10<sup>9</sup> W?`,['3 MW','3 GW','300 MW','30 GW'],1,`Giga means 10<sup>9</sup>, so 3 × 10<sup>9</sup> W = 3 GW. That's about the heat output of one large reactor core.`),
T(`<h3>Prefixes</h3><table><tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Multiply by</th></tr><tr><td>nano</td><td>n</td><td>10<sup>−9</sup></td></tr><tr><td>micro</td><td>µ</td><td>10<sup>−6</sup></td></tr><tr><td>milli</td><td>m</td><td>10<sup>−3</sup></td></tr><tr><td>kilo</td><td>k</td><td>10<sup>3</sup></td></tr><tr><td>mega</td><td>M</td><td>10<sup>6</sup></td></tr><tr><td>giga</td><td>G</td><td>10<sup>9</sup></td></tr></table><p>On a calculator, use the <b>×10<sup>x</sup></b> or <b>EXP</b> button.</p>`),
N(`A source range detector reads 4.5 × 10<sup>4</sup> counts per second. Write that as an ordinary number.`,45000,0.001,'counts per second',`10<sup>4</sup> = 10,000, so 4.5 × 10<sup>4</sup> = 45,000.`),
T(`<h3>A unit you'll use every shift: pcm</h3><p>You'll meet <b>reactivity</b> properly in unit 4. For now, all you need to know is that it's a small fraction, written Δk/k.</p><p>Because the numbers are so small, operators use <b>pcm</b> ("per cent mille"):</p><div class="eq">1 pcm = 10<sup>−5</sup> Δk/k = 0.001 %Δk/k</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">pcm</span><span class="km">“per cent mille”: one hundred-thousandth</span><span class="ku">1 pcm = 0.00001</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">Δk/k</span><span class="km">the fractional change in k (Δ means “change in”)</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">%Δk/k</span><span class="km">the same thing written as a percentage</span><span class="ku">%</span></div></div>`),
W('unitConvert',{target:250},`Move the slider until the reactivity is <b>0.25 %Δk/k</b>. How many pcm is that?`,`0.25 % = 0.0025 Δk/k = 250 pcm. To convert Δk/k to pcm, multiply by 100,000.`),
N(`A boron change adds 0.0042 Δk/k. How many pcm is that?`,420,0.01,'pcm',`0.0042 × 100,000 = 420 pcm.`),
Q(`Which is the biggest reactivity?`,['500 pcm','0.004 Δk/k','0.45 %Δk/k','4 × 10<sup>−3</sup> Δk/k'],0,`Convert everything to pcm: 500, 400, 450 and 400. So 500 pcm is the biggest.`)]),
L('m2','Exponential growth',10,[
T(`<h3>Power multiplies</h3><p>When reactor power rises, it doesn't go up by the same <i>amount</i> each second. It goes up by the same <i>percentage</i>. That means it multiplies, which is called <b>exponential growth</b>.</p><p>Exponential growth starts slowly, then becomes explosive. Let's feel how fast.</p>`),
W('doubling',{},`Start at 1 watt. Keep doubling until you reach <b>1 gigawatt</b> (10<sup>9</sup> W), the scale of a reactor.`,`It only takes 30 doublings to go from 1 W to over 1 GW. If power doubled every minute, that's half an hour. That's why rates of change matter so much in a control room.`),
Q(`How many doublings take you from 1 W to over 1 GW?`,['About 10','About 30','About 300','About 1,000,000'],1,`2<sup>30</sup> ≈ 1.07 × 10<sup>9</sup>. Ten doublings multiply by about 1000, so thirty multiply by about 1000 × 1000 × 1000.`),
T(`<h3>The number e and the reactor period</h3><p>Physicists write exponential growth using the number <b>e ≈ 2.718</b>:</p><div class="eq">P = P<sub>0</sub> e<sup>t/τ</sup></div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">P</span><span class="km">reactor power right now</span><span class="ku">watts (W), or % of full power</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">P<sub>0</sub></span><span class="km">the power you started at</span><span class="ku">same unit as P</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">e</span><span class="km">a special number, about 2.718 (it’s on your calculator as e<sup>x</sup>)</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">t</span><span class="km">time since you started</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">τ</span><span class="km">“tau”, the reactor <b>period</b>: the time for power to grow by a factor of e (×2.718)</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div></div><p>τ (tau) is the <b>reactor period</b>: the time for power to grow by a factor of e. A short period means a fast rise.</p>`),
N(`Power starts at 1% and the reactor period is τ = 60 s. What's the power after 60 s?`,2.718,0.02,'%',`After one period, power multiplies by e: 1% × 2.718 = 2.72%.`),
W('periodPlot',{lo:47,hi:57},`Find the period that takes power from 1% to 10% in about <b>2 minutes</b> (120 s).`,`Power rises ×10 when t/τ = ln 10 = 2.30, so τ = 120 ÷ 2.30 ≈ 52 s.`),
T(`<h3>Doubling time</h3><p>Power doubles every <b>0.693 τ</b> (because e<sup>0.693</sup> = 2). So a 100 s period means power doubles every 69 s.</p>`),
N(`The reactor period is 100 s. What's the doubling time?`,69.3,0.02,'s',`Doubling time = 0.693 × 100 = 69.3 s.`),
Q(`Which period would make an operator act straight away?`,['500 s','100 s','60 s','5 s'],3,`A 5 s period means power multiplies by e every 5 seconds: ×10 in about 11.5 s. Controlled startups use periods of around a minute or longer.`)]),
L('m3','Logarithms and log scales',10,[
T(`<h3>"What power?"</h3><p>A logarithm answers the question "what power?". log<sub>10</sub>(1000) = 3 because 10<sup>3</sup> = 1000.</p><p><b>ln</b> is the logarithm to base e. It undoes e<sup>x</sup>: ln(e<sup>x</sup>) = x.</p>`),
N(`What is log<sub>10</sub>(10<sup>6</sup>)?`,6,0.001,'',`10 to the power 6 is 10<sup>6</sup>, so the log is 6.`),
T(`<h3>Why control rooms use log scales</h3><p>Neutron power spans about <b>11 decades</b>: from around 10<sup>−9</sup>% when shut down to 100%. A normal scale can't show that. On a <b>log scale</b>, each decade (each ×10) takes up the same space.</p>`),
W('logMeter',{target:3e4},`Set the source range meter to <b>3 × 10<sup>4</sup></b> counts per second. Watch what the ordinary (linear) meter does.`,`On the log meter, 3 × 10<sup>4</sup> sits about halfway between 10<sup>4</sup> and 10<sup>5</sup>, because log<sub>10</sub>3 ≈ 0.48. The linear meter can barely show anything below about 10<sup>4</sup>.`),
Q(`On a log meter, the needle moves from 10<sup>2</sup> to 10<sup>3</sup> cps. It then moves the same distance again. What does it read now?`,['1100 cps','2000 cps','10<sup>4</sup> cps','5000 cps'],2,`Equal distances on a log scale mean equal <i>multiplications</i>. ×10 then ×10 again: 10<sup>4</sup> cps.`),
T(`<h3>Solving for time</h3><p>Logs let you find how long something takes. If P = P<sub>0</sub> e<sup>t/τ</sup>, then:</p><div class="eq">t = τ × ln(P / P<sub>0</sub>)</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">t</span><span class="km">time needed to get from P<sub>0</sub> to P</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">τ</span><span class="km">the reactor period</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">ln</span><span class="km">natural log: the button on your calculator that undoes e<sup>x</sup></span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">P, P<sub>0</sub></span><span class="km">final and starting power</span><span class="ku">same unit as each other</span></div></div>`),
N(`How long does power take to rise from 10<sup>−6</sup>% to 10<sup>−1</sup>% on a 60 s period?`,690.8,0.02,'s',`t = 60 × ln(10<sup>5</sup>) = 60 × 11.51 = 691 s, or about 11.5 minutes.`),
Q(`Power is rising at 1 decade per minute. How long to go from 10<sup>−8</sup>% to 10<sup>−2</sup>%?`,['6 minutes','2 minutes','60 minutes','1 minute'],0,`That's 6 decades (from 10<sup>−8</sup> to 10<sup>−2</sup>), at 1 decade per minute: 6 minutes. Operators measure startup rate in decades per minute, as you'll see in unit 5.`)]),
L('m4','Rates of change',8,[
T(`<h3>How fast is it changing?</h3><p>A <b>rate of change</b> is how fast something is changing right now. On a graph, it's the <b>gradient</b> (steepness) of the curve at that point.</p><p>Operators care about rates as much as values: a temperature of 300 °C is fine, but 300 °C and rising 5 °C per second is not.</p>`),
W('tangent',{},`This graph shows power during a slow rise. Move the slider to find where power is <b>rising fastest</b>.`,`The gradient is greatest where the curve is steepest, in the middle of the rise. The tangent line shows the gradient at each point.`),
T(`<h3>Notation</h3><div class="eq">dP/dt</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">dP</span><span class="km">a tiny change in power</span><span class="ku">W or %</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">dt</span><span class="km">the tiny bit of time it happens in</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">dP/dt</span><span class="km">how fast power is changing: change in power per second</span><span class="ku">W/s or %/s</span></div><div class="kn">Read “dP/dt” as “the rate of change of P”. It’s a gradient, not a fraction you cancel.</div></div><p>means "the rate of change of P with respect to time". If power P is in % and time t is in seconds, dP/dt is in % per second.</p>`),
Q(`A tank level graph is perfectly flat. What is d(level)/dt?`,['0','Positive','Negative','Infinite'],0,`Flat means not changing, so the rate of change is zero.`),
T(`<h3>Exponential growth has a special rate</h3><p>For exponential growth, the rate of change is proportional to the amount:</p><div class="eq">dP/dt = P / τ</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">dP/dt</span><span class="km">how fast power is changing</span><span class="ku">W/s or %/s</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">P</span><span class="km">power right now</span><span class="ku">W or %</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">τ</span><span class="km">the reactor period</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div></div><p>The more power there is, the faster it grows. That's what makes exponentials run away.</p>`),
N(`Power is 50% and the period is 100 s. How fast is power rising right now?`,0.5,0.02,'% per second',`dP/dt = P/τ = 50/100 = 0.5% per second.`),
Q(`Coolant temperature is falling at 0.5 °C every second. Which is dT/dt?`,['+0.5 °C/s','−0.5 °C/s','0.5 s/°C','−2 °C/s'],1,`Falling means the rate is negative: −0.5 °C per second.`)]),
L('m5','Differential equations',10,[
T(`<h3>Equations about change</h3><p>A <b>differential equation</b> describes how something changes. For example:</p><div class="eq">dN/dt = −λN</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">dN/dt</span><span class="km">how fast the number of nuclei is changing (per second)</span><span class="ku">nuclei per second</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">λ</span><span class="km">the decay constant</span><span class="ku">per second (s<sup>−1</sup>)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">N</span><span class="km">number of nuclei</span><span class="ku">a count</span></div><div class="kn">The minus sign means N is going <b>down</b>.</div></div><p>says "nuclei disappear at a rate proportional to how many are left". λ is a constant.</p>`),
W('euler',{},`Step the equation forward in time yourself. Each step uses the current rate to predict the next value. Try a big step size, then a small one, and compare with the exact curve.`,null),
T(`<h3>The exact answer</h3><p>With calculus (A-level maths), you can solve dN/dt = −λN exactly:</p><div class="eq">N = N<sub>0</sub> e<sup>−λt</sup></div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">N</span><span class="km">number of radioactive nuclei left</span><span class="ku">a count (no unit)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">N<sub>0</sub></span><span class="km">number you started with</span><span class="ku">a count</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">e</span><span class="km">a special number, about 2.718</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">λ</span><span class="km">“lambda”, the <b>decay constant</b>: the fraction that decays each second</span><span class="ku">per second (s<sup>−1</sup>)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">t</span><span class="km">time</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div></div><p>Smaller steps get closer to this curve. Computers simulating reactors use exactly this idea, with very small steps.</p>`),
Q(`Which equation gives exponential growth?`,['dP/dt = 5','dP/dt = P/τ','dP/dt = −t','dP/dt = 0'],1,`Growth proportional to P itself always gives an exponential. dP/dt = 5 gives a straight line.`),
N(`dN/dt = −0.05N, and right now N = 400. What is dN/dt?`,-20,0.01,'per second',`−0.05 × 400 = −20. Negative, so N is falling.`),
T(`<h3>Where this leads</h3><p>Reactor kinetics uses equations like this one:</p><div class="eq">dn/dt = ((ρ − β)/Λ) n + λC</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">n</span><span class="km">number of neutrons in the core</span><span class="ku">a count (or neutrons per cm³)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">ρ</span><span class="km">reactivity</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">β</span><span class="km">“beta”, the fraction of neutrons that are <b>delayed</b> (about 0.0065)</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">Λ</span><span class="km">“capital lambda”, time from one neutron generation to the next</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">λ</span><span class="km">decay constant of the atoms that give off delayed neutrons</span><span class="ku">per second (s<sup>−1</sup>)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">C</span><span class="km">number of those atoms waiting to decay</span><span class="ku">a count</span></div><div class="kn">You don’t need to solve this one: just see that it says “change in neutrons depends on reactivity and on delayed neutrons”.</div></div><p>It looks scary, but it's the same idea: the rate of change of neutrons depends on how many there are. You'll use it in unit 5 and in the control room.</p>`),
Q(`If dN/dt = kN and k is positive, what happens to N?`,['It falls to zero','It grows exponentially','It stays constant','It grows in a straight line'],1,`A positive rate proportional to N means growth that keeps speeding up: exponential growth.`)]),
L('m6','Counting statistics',8,[
T(`<h3>Randomness in the readings</h3><p>Radioactive decay is random, and so is neutron detection at low power. A detector averaging 100 counts per second won't show exactly 100 every second.</p>`),
W('poisson',{},`Choose an average count rate, then press <b>Count</b> to take fifty 1-second counts. Compare how spread out the results are for low and high rates.`,null),
T(`<h3>The square-root rule</h3><p>For random counts, the typical spread (standard deviation) is about <b>√N</b>:</p><table><tr><th>Counts</th><th>Spread</th><th>Relative spread</th></tr><tr><td>100</td><td>±10</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td>10,000</td><td>±100</td><td>1%</td></tr></table><p>More counts means a more precise reading.</p>`),
N(`You record 2500 counts. What's the standard deviation?`,50,0.01,'counts',`√2500 = 50 counts, which is 2% of the total.`),
Q(`At very low power, the source range reading flickers up and down. Is the reactor unstable?`,['Yes: the flicker means it\'s out of control, so trip it','Not necessarily: low count rates fluctuate randomly','Yes: power is oscillating between high and low values','No: a flickering reading means the detector is broken'],1,`At low count rates the random scatter is large compared with the reading. Operators look at the trend over time, not every flicker.`),
Q(`Near criticality, why do operators wait and average count rates over longer periods?`,['To give the detector time to warm up, so its readings become steady and accurate enough to trust','Longer counts give more total counts, so random scatter is smaller relative to the reading','Because the reactor gets hotter near criticality, so the counts drift','The turbine needs time to speed up before the count rate can be read'],1,`A 100 s count gives 100 times more counts than a 1 s count, so the relative scatter is 10 times smaller.`)])
]});

U({id:'energy',title:'Energy and matter',icon:'E',color:'#378ADD',desc:'Energy and power, collisions and momentum, and E = mc².',lessons:[
L('e1','Energy, work and power',8,[
T(`<h3>Joules and watts</h3><p>Energy is measured in <b>joules</b> (J). Power is how fast energy is transferred: <b>1 watt = 1 joule per second</b>.</p><div class="eq">energy = power × time</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">energy</span><span class="km">total energy transferred</span><span class="ku">joules (J)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">power</span><span class="km">energy per second</span><span class="ku">watts (W) = joules per second</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">time</span><span class="km">how long</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div><div class="kn">Use seconds, not hours, or the answer won’t be in joules.</div></div>`),
N(`A reactor makes 3000 MW of heat for one hour. How much energy is that, in terajoules (TJ = 10<sup>12</sup> J)?`,10.8,0.01,'TJ',`3 × 10<sup>9</sup> W × 3600 s = 1.08 × 10<sup>13</sup> J = 10.8 TJ.`),
T(`<h3>MWth and MWe</h3><p>A reactor's heat output is its <b>thermal power</b>, in MWth. The turbine and generator turn part of that into <b>electrical power</b>, in MWe. The rest leaves as warm water through the condenser.</p><div class="eq">efficiency = MWe ÷ MWth</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">MWe</span><span class="km">megawatts of <b>electricity</b> sent out</span><span class="ku">MW (1 MW = 10<sup>6</sup> W)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">MWth</span><span class="km">megawatts of <b>heat</b> made in the reactor</span><span class="ku">MW</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">efficiency</span><span class="km">fraction of the heat turned into electricity</span><span class="ku">no unit (× 100 for %)</span></div></div>`),
W('efficiency',{},`A Hinkley Point C reactor makes about <b>4590 MWth</b> of heat and about <b>1630 MWe</b> of electricity. Set the sliders to match, then read off the efficiency.`,`About 35%. Steam power stations can't turn all their heat into electricity: unit 6 explains why.`),
N(`A 3400 MWth reactor runs at 35% efficiency. What's its electrical output?`,1190,0.01,'MWe',`3400 × 0.35 = 1190 MWe.`),
Q(`Where does most of a nuclear power station's heat end up?`,['As electricity sent out along the grid to homes and businesses','As warm cooling water (the sea or cooling towers)','Stored in the used fuel for years','As radiation absorbed by the shielding'],1,`About two thirds of the heat goes out through the condenser into cooling water.`)]),
L('e2','Collisions and momentum',8,[
T(`<h3>Bouncing neutrons</h3><p>Momentum p = mv. In any collision, total momentum is conserved. In an <b>elastic</b> collision, kinetic energy is conserved too.</p><p>Neutrons bouncing off nuclei are (almost) elastic collisions, and that's how reactors slow neutrons down.</p>`),
W('collision',{},`A fast neutron hits a nucleus head-on. Find the nucleus that takes <b>the most energy</b> from the neutron in one collision.`,`Hydrogen-1 has almost exactly the neutron's mass, so a head-on collision can stop the neutron dead, like one snooker ball hitting another.`),
T(`<h3>The mass matters</h3><p>After a head-on collision with a nucleus of mass number A, the neutron keeps this fraction of its energy:</p><div class="eq">((A − 1)/(A + 1))<sup>2</sup></div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">A</span><span class="km">mass number of the nucleus hit: protons + neutrons</span><span class="ku">no unit (hydrogen 1, carbon 12)</span></div><div class="kn">The answer is the fraction of its energy the neutron <b>keeps</b> after a head-on hit.</div></div><p>Heavy nuclei barely slow it: off uranium-238, a neutron keeps over 98% of its energy.</p>`),
N(`For a head-on collision with carbon-12, what fraction of its energy does the neutron keep? Answer as a decimal.`,0.716,0.01,'',`(11/13)<sup>2</sup> = 0.716. So it loses about 28%: carbon (graphite) is a moderator, but a much slower one than water.`),
Q(`Why are light nuclei good at slowing neutrons down?`,['They absorb neutrons, which stops them moving so fast','Their mass is close to the neutron\'s, so each collision takes a lot of energy','They\'re radioactive, so they give off energy that pushes against the neutrons and slows them','Their mass is much bigger, so the neutron bounces off slowly'],1,`Energy transfer is biggest when the masses are similar. That's why reactors use hydrogen (water), deuterium (heavy water) or carbon (graphite) as moderators.`)]),
L('e3','E = mc², part 1: what the letters mean',8,[
T(`<h3>The big idea first</h3><p>Before any maths, here's the whole idea in one sentence:</p><div class="callout"><b>Mass can turn into energy, and a tiny bit of mass makes an enormous amount of energy.</b></div><p>That's all E = mc² says. It's why one small uranium pellet can give as much energy as a tonne of coal.</p><p>We'll build it up slowly over four short lessons:</p><ol class="steps"><li><b>This lesson:</b> what each letter means, and what it's measured in.</li><li>Putting real numbers in.</li><li>The special small units used for atoms.</li><li>Using it to find where a reactor's energy comes from.</li></ol>`),
W('emcLetters',{},`Here's the equation. <b>Tap each part</b> to see what it means and what it's measured in.`,`Four parts: <b>E</b> is energy, <b>m</b> is mass, <b>c</b> is the speed of light, and the little <b>²</b> means "multiply c by itself".`),
Q(`In E = mc², what does <b>m</b> stand for?`,[`The mass that turns into energy`,'The number of atoms that split','The distance the energy travels','The momentum of the particles'],0,`m is <b>mass</b>: the tiny amount of mass that turns into energy. (Careful: the unit symbol "m" means metres, but the letter m in the equation means mass.)`,`It's measured in kilograms.`),
T(`<h3>Units: what each thing is measured in</h3><p>Every quantity comes with a <b>unit</b>: the thing you count it in. Distance can be in miles or metres; time in hours or seconds.</p><p>E = mc² only gives the right answer if you use these units:</p><table><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Means</th><th>Unit</th><th>Short form</th></tr><tr><td><b>E</b></td><td>energy</td><td>joule</td><td>J</td></tr><tr><td><b>m</b></td><td>mass</td><td>kilogram</td><td>kg</td></tr><tr><td><b>c</b></td><td>speed of light</td><td>metres per second</td><td>m/s</td></tr></table><div class="callout">Put the mass in <b>kilograms</b> (not grams) and the energy comes out in <b>joules</b>.</div>`),
Q(`You want the energy in joules. What unit must the mass be in?`,[`Kilograms (kg)`,`Grams (g)`,`Tonnes`,`Any unit works`],0,`Kilograms. If you're given grams, divide by 1000 first: 5 g = 0.005 kg.`),
W('joules',{},`How big is a joule? Slide up through the examples to get a feel for it. Keep going until you reach <b>1 gram of mass</b>.`,`A joule is tiny: about the energy to lift an apple 1 metre. But 1 gram of mass is worth about <b>90 trillion joules</b>, more than 9,000 UK homes use in a year.`),
T(`<h3>Meet c, the speed of light</h3><p>c is always the same number. Light travels 300 million metres every second:</p><div class="eq">c = 300,000,000 m/s = 3.00 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">c</span><span class="km">the speed of light</span><span class="ku">metres per second (m/s)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">10<sup>8</sup></span><span class="km">1 followed by 8 zeros = 100,000,000</span><span class="ku"></span></div></div><p>"× 10<sup>8</sup>" is just a short way to write "× 100,000,000" (a 1 followed by 8 zeros). That's fast enough to go round the Earth about 7 times in one second.</p>`),
Q(`3.00 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s is the same as…`,[`300 million metres per second`,`3 million metres per second`,`30,000 metres per second`,`3 billion metres per second`],0,`10<sup>8</sup> = 100,000,000 (8 zeros). 3 × 100,000,000 = 300,000,000: 300 million.`,`Count the zeros: 10<sup>8</sup> has 8 of them.`),
T(`<h3>Why c² makes the answer so big</h3><p>The little ² means "squared": multiply c by itself. Step by step:</p><ol class="steps"><li>c<sup>2</sup> = c × c = 3.00 × 10<sup>8</sup> × 3.00 × 10<sup>8</sup></li><li>Multiply the front numbers: 3 × 3 = <b>9</b></li><li>Add the powers of ten: 10<sup>8</sup> × 10<sup>8</sup> = 10<sup>8 + 8</sup> = <b>10<sup>16</sup></b></li><li>So c<sup>2</sup> = <b>9 × 10<sup>16</sup></b></li></ol><p>That's 90,000,000,000,000,000. Whatever mass you put in gets multiplied by this gigantic number, which is why a tiny mass gives a huge energy.</p>`),
N(`Work out c² for yourself. Give the number in front of × 10<sup>16</sup>.`,9,0.01,'× 10¹⁶',`3 × 3 = 9, and 10<sup>8</sup> × 10<sup>8</sup> = 10<sup>16</sup>. So c² = 9 × 10<sup>16</sup>.`,`Multiply the front numbers (3 × 3), then add the powers (8 + 8).`),
T(`<h3>Do the units fit?</h3><p>Try the units instead of numbers:</p><ol class="steps"><li>m is in <b>kg</b>, and c<sup>2</sup> is in <b>(m/s)<sup>2</sup> = m<sup>2</sup>/s<sup>2</sup></b>.</li><li>Multiply them: kg × m<sup>2</sup>/s<sup>2</sup> = <b>kg·m<sup>2</sup>/s<sup>2</sup></b>.</li><li>And that's exactly what a joule is: 1 J = 1 kg·m<sup>2</sup>/s<sup>2</sup>.</li></ol><div class="callout">So kilograms × (metres per second)<sup>2</sup> gives joules. The units fit, which is how you know the equation is being used properly.</div>`),
Q(`Why does a tiny mass give such a huge energy?`,[`Because it's multiplied by c², which is 9 × 10<sup>16</sup>`,'Because the atoms involved are very heavy','Because joules are such big units of energy','Because the mass is measured in grams, not kg'],0,`c² is a gigantic number, so even a fraction of a gram, multiplied by 9 × 10<sup>16</sup>, gives billions of joules.`)]),

L('e3b','E = mc², part 2: putting numbers in',8,[
T(`<h3>A recipe that works every time</h3><p>You know what the letters mean. Now let's use it. Three steps, always the same:</p><ol class="steps"><li><b>Get the mass in kilograms.</b> Grams? Divide by 1000.</li><li><b>Square c.</b> It's always 9 × 10<sup>16</sup>.</li><li><b>Multiply them.</b> The answer is the energy in joules.</li></ol><div class="eq">E = mc<sup>2</sup></div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">E</span><span class="km">energy released</span><span class="ku">joules (J)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">m</span><span class="km">mass turned into energy</span><span class="ku">kilograms (kg)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">c<sup>2</sup></span><span class="km">speed of light × itself = 9 × 10<sup>16</sup></span><span class="ku">(m/s)²</span></div></div>`),
T(`<h3>Worked example: 1 kilogram</h3><ol class="steps"><li>Mass: <b>1 kg</b> (already in kilograms).</li><li>c<sup>2</sup> = <b>9 × 10<sup>16</sup></b></li><li>E = 1 × 9 × 10<sup>16</sup> = <b>9 × 10<sup>16</sup> J</b></li></ol><p>That's roughly four months of electricity for every home in the UK, from one kilogram of mass.</p>`),
N(`Use the recipe for <b>2 kg</b> of mass. Give the number in front of × 10<sup>16</sup> J.`,18,0.01,'× 10¹⁶ J',`E = 2 × 9 × 10<sup>16</sup> = 18 × 10<sup>16</sup> J (also written 1.8 × 10<sup>17</sup> J).`,`It's already in kilograms, so just do 2 × 9.`),
T(`<h3>Worked example: 1 gram</h3><p>Grams need one extra step at the start.</p><ol class="steps"><li>Convert to kilograms: 1 g ÷ 1000 = <b>0.001 kg</b>, which is 1 × 10<sup>−3</sup> kg.</li><li>c<sup>2</sup> = <b>9 × 10<sup>16</sup></b></li><li>Multiply the front numbers: 1 × 9 = <b>9</b></li><li>Add the powers: 10<sup>−3</sup> × 10<sup>16</sup> = 10<sup>−3 + 16</sup> = <b>10<sup>13</sup></b></li><li>E = <b>9 × 10<sup>13</sup> J</b></li></ol><div class="callout">A negative power (like 10<sup>−3</sup>) means a small number: 10<sup>−3</sup> = 1/1000 = 0.001.</div>`),
W('emc2',{},`Slide the mass and watch the recipe work, with real comparisons. Set it to <b>1 gram</b>.`,`1 gram gives 9 × 10<sup>13</sup> J: enough for about 9,000 UK homes for a year, or about 5½ hours of one Hinkley Point C reactor at full power.`),
N(`Now try <b>5 grams</b>. Give the number in front of × 10<sup>14</sup> J.`,4.5,0.01,'× 10¹⁴ J',`5 g = 0.005 kg. E = 0.005 × 9 × 10<sup>16</sup> = 0.045 × 10<sup>16</sup> = 4.5 × 10<sup>14</sup> J.`,`Step 1: 5 g = 0.005 kg. Step 2: 0.005 × 9 = 0.045. Then 0.045 × 10<sup>16</sup> = 4.5 × 10<sup>14</sup>.`),
T(`<h3>Where does the mass go?</h3><p>It doesn't vanish into nothing. When energy is released (as heat, light or fast-moving particles), it carries the mass away with it.</p><p>In a reactor, the pieces left after fission are very slightly lighter than what you started with. That "missing" mass has become heat in the fuel. The heat boils water, and the steam spins the turbine.</p><div class="callout">The mass involved is always tiny. A big reactor running flat out for a whole year turns only about <b>1–2 kg</b> of mass into energy.</div>`),
T(`<h3>Going backwards: finding the mass</h3><p>Sometimes you know the energy and want the mass. Rearrange E = mc² by dividing both sides by c<sup>2</sup>:</p><div class="eq">m = E ÷ c<sup>2</sup></div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">m</span><span class="km">mass converted</span><span class="ku">kilograms (kg)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">E</span><span class="km">energy released</span><span class="ku">joules (J)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">c<sup>2</sup></span><span class="km">9 × 10<sup>16</sup></span><span class="ku">(m/s)²</span></div></div><p>Example: a reactor makes 9 × 10<sup>16</sup> J of heat. m = 9 × 10<sup>16</sup> ÷ 9 × 10<sup>16</sup> = <b>1 kg</b> of mass converted.</p>`),
N(`A reaction releases 1.8 × 10<sup>14</sup> J. How much mass was converted, in <b>grams</b>?`,2,0.01,'g',`m = 1.8 × 10<sup>14</sup> ÷ 9 × 10<sup>16</sup> = 0.2 × 10<sup>−2</sup> = 0.002 kg = <b>2 g</b>.`,`Divide the front numbers (1.8 ÷ 9 = 0.2) and subtract the powers (14 − 16 = −2). Then turn kg into grams (× 1000).`),
Q(`In a power station, what does the "missing" mass become?`,[`Heat in the fuel, which makes steam`,'Nothing: the mass is simply destroyed','New uranium, which goes back into the fuel','Electricity, sent straight into the grid'],0,`The energy appears as heat in the fuel. That heat boils water, the steam turns the turbine, and the generator makes electricity.`)]),

L('e3c','E = mc², part 3: units for tiny things',10,[
T(`<h3>The problem with atoms</h3><p>A single atom is unbelievably light. One neutron weighs:</p><div class="eq"><span style="white-space:nowrap;font-size:.7em">0.000 000 000 000 000 000 000 000 001 67 kg</span><br>= 1.67 × 10<sup>−27</sup> kg</div><p>The minus sign in the power means a <b>tiny</b> number: divide by 10, twenty-seven times.</p><p>Working with numbers like that for every atom is painful, so physicists use units made for atoms. There are just two to learn.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Unit 1: the atomic mass unit, u</h3><div class="eq">1 u = 1.66 × 10<sup>−27</sup> kg</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">u</span><span class="km">atomic mass unit: about the mass of one proton or neutron</span><span class="ku">a unit of mass</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">10<sup>−27</sup></span><span class="km">divide by 10, twenty-seven times: a tiny number</span><span class="ku"></span></div></div><p>One <b>atomic mass unit</b> is about the mass of one proton or one neutron. Atom masses become friendly numbers:</p><table><tr><th>Particle</th><th>Mass</th></tr><tr><td>neutron</td><td>1.0087 u</td></tr><tr><td>uranium-235 atom</td><td>235.0439 u</td></tr><tr><td>barium-141 atom</td><td>140.9144 u</td></tr></table><div class="callout">Handy pattern: the mass in u is very close to the number in the atom's name. Uranium-<b>235</b> ≈ <b>235</b> u.</div>`),
Q(`Roughly what is the mass of a uranium-238 atom?`,[`238 u`,`92 u`,`1 u`,`238 kg`],0,`About 238 u: the number in the name is the number of protons plus neutrons, and each weighs about 1 u.`),
T(`<h3>Unit 2: the electronvolt, eV</h3><p>For one atom, joules are far too big, like weighing a grain of sand in tonnes. So we use the <b>electronvolt</b> (eV):</p><div class="eq">1 eV = 1.60 × 10<sup>−19</sup> J</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">eV</span><span class="km">electronvolt: a tiny unit of energy for single particles</span><span class="ku">a unit of energy</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">J</span><span class="km">joule</span><span class="ku">a unit of energy</span></div></div><p>Nuclear reactions release <b>millions</b> of eV, so we usually use the <b>mega-electronvolt</b> (MeV). "Mega" means a million:</p><div class="eq">1 MeV = 1,000,000 eV = 1.60 × 10<sup>−13</sup> J</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">MeV</span><span class="km">mega-electronvolt: a million eV</span><span class="ku">a unit of energy</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">M (mega)</span><span class="km">a million = 10<sup>6</sup></span><span class="ku"></span></div></div><p>(Where −13 comes from: 10<sup>6</sup> × 10<sup>−19</sup> = 10<sup>6 − 19</sup> = 10<sup>−13</sup>.)</p>`),
W('eunits',{},`Type an energy and pick its unit, and you'll see it in all three units at once. Find out how many joules <b>200 MeV</b> is (the energy of one fission).`,`200 MeV = 3.2 × 10<sup>−11</sup> J. Tiny for one atom, but a reactor splits about 10<sup>20</sup> atoms every second.`),
N(`Convert <b>2 MeV</b> to joules. Give the number in front of × 10<sup>−13</sup> J.`,3.2,0.01,'× 10⁻¹³ J',`2 × 1.60 × 10<sup>−13</sup> = 3.2 × 10<sup>−13</sup> J.`,`Each MeV is 1.60 × 10<sup>−13</sup> J. You have 2 of them.`),
W('u931',{},`Now the clever bit: how much energy is <b>1 u</b> of mass worth? Use E = mc² one step at a time. Press <b>Next step</b> until you reach the answer.`,`<b>1 u of mass = 931.5 MeV of energy.</b> Work it out once and you never have to again.`),
T(`<h3>The shortcut</h3><div class="eq">energy in MeV = mass in u × 931.5</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">931.5</span><span class="km">the energy (in MeV) that 1 u of mass is worth</span><span class="ku">MeV per u</span></div></div><p>This <i>is</i> E = mc², just in atom-sized units, so all the powers of ten have already been done for you.</p><ol class="steps"><li>Find the mass in u.</li><li>Multiply by 931.5.</li><li>The answer is in MeV.</li></ol>`),
N(`A nuclear reaction loses <b>0.1 u</b> of mass. How much energy does it release?`,93.15,0.01,'MeV',`0.1 × 931.5 = 93.15 MeV.`,`Mass in u × 931.5.`),
N(`Another loses <b>0.02 u</b>. How much energy, in MeV?`,18.63,0.01,'MeV',`0.02 × 931.5 = 18.63 MeV.`),
Q(`Which of these is E = mc² written for atoms?`,[`MeV = u × 931.5`,`J = u × 931.5`,`MeV = u ÷ 931.5`,`u = MeV × 931.5`],0,`Mass in atomic mass units, times 931.5, gives energy in MeV.`)]),

L('e3d','E = mc², part 4: the energy in fission',10,[
T(`<h3>Weigh it before, weigh it after</h3><p>Now everything comes together. Where does a reactor's energy actually come from? Let's weigh everything before and after one uranium nucleus splits.</p><p>Here's one typical fission. A neutron hits uranium-235, which splits into barium-141, krypton-92 and 3 new neutrons:</p><div class="eq">n + U-235 → Ba-141 + Kr-92 + 3n</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">n</span><span class="km">a neutron</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">U-235</span><span class="km">uranium-235 (235 = protons + neutrons)</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">Ba-141</span><span class="km">barium-141</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">Kr-92</span><span class="km">krypton-92</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">3n</span><span class="km">three new neutrons</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">→</span><span class="km">“turns into”</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="kn">Check: 1 + 235 = 141 + 92 + 3 = 236. The particle count balances, but the <b>mass</b> doesn’t quite.</div></div>`),
W('fissionScale',{},`Put "before" on one side of the scales and "after" on the other. Press <b>Split it</b>.`,`The pieces after fission weigh <b>less</b> than what you started with. The difference is 0.186 u.`),
T(`<h3>Adding up the masses</h3><table><tr><th>Before</th><th>Mass</th></tr><tr><td>uranium-235</td><td>235.0439 u</td></tr><tr><td>1 neutron</td><td>1.0087 u</td></tr><tr><td><b>Total</b></td><td><b>236.0526 u</b></td></tr></table><table><tr><th>After</th><th>Mass</th></tr><tr><td>barium-141</td><td>140.9144 u</td></tr><tr><td>krypton-92</td><td>91.9262 u</td></tr><tr><td>3 neutrons (3 × 1.0087)</td><td>3.0260 u</td></tr><tr><td><b>Total</b></td><td><b>235.8666 u</b></td></tr></table><p>Missing mass = before − after.</p>`),
N(`Work out the missing mass: 236.0526 − 235.8666 = ?`,0.186,0.01,'u',`236.0526 − 235.8666 = <b>0.186 u</b>. That mass has turned into energy.`,`Take the "after" total away from the "before" total.`),
T(`<h3>Turn the missing mass into energy</h3><p>Use the shortcut from part 3:</p><ol class="steps"><li>Missing mass = <b>0.186 u</b></li><li>Energy = 0.186 × 931.5</li></ol><p>The fragments fly apart at enormous speed, crash into the atoms around them in the fuel, and heat it up. That heat is what the reactor is for.</p><div class="callout">A little more energy (about 27 MeV) comes out later as the fragments decay, so each fission gives about <b>200 MeV</b> in total.</div>`),
N(`How much energy is 0.186 u? (× 931.5)`,173.3,0.01,'MeV',`0.186 × 931.5 = <b>173 MeV</b>, released the moment the nucleus splits.`),
T(`<h3>From MeV to joules</h3><p>To compare with everyday energy, convert one fission's 200 MeV to joules:</p><ol class="steps"><li>1 MeV = 1.60 × 10<sup>−13</sup> J</li><li>200 × 1.60 = <b>320</b>, so 200 MeV = 320 × 10<sup>−13</sup> J</li><li>Tidy it: 320 × 10<sup>−13</sup> = <b>3.2 × 10<sup>−11</sup> J</b></li></ol>`),
N(`Convert 200 MeV to joules. Give the number in front of × 10<sup>−11</sup> J.`,3.2,0.02,'× 10⁻¹¹ J',`200 × 1.60 × 10<sup>−13</sup> = 3.2 × 10<sup>−11</sup> J.`,`Multiply 200 by 1.60 first, then sort out the power of ten.`),
T(`<h3>Nuclear vs chemical</h3><p>Burning fuel is a <b>chemical</b> reaction: atoms swap partners, and only the electrons are involved. Burning one carbon atom releases about <b>4 eV</b>.</p><p>One fission releases about <b>200 MeV</b> = 200,000,000 eV.</p><div class="callout">200,000,000 ÷ 4 = <b>50 million</b>. Each fission gives about 50 million times the energy of burning one atom of coal. That's why a reactor needs so little fuel.</div>`),
Q(`Roughly how many times more energy does one fission release than burning one carbon atom?`,[`About 50 million times`,`About 50 times`,`About 50 thousand times`,`The same`],0,`200 MeV ÷ 4 eV = 200,000,000 ÷ 4 = 50,000,000.`),
T(`<h3>How many fissions make 1 watt?</h3><p>1 watt means 1 joule every second. Each fission gives 3.2 × 10<sup>−11</sup> J, so:</p><div class="eq">fissions per second = 1 ÷ 3.2 × 10<sup>−11</sup> = 3.1 × 10<sup>10</sup></div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">1</span><span class="km">1 watt = 1 joule every second</span><span class="ku">J/s</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">3.2 × 10<sup>−11</sup></span><span class="km">energy from one fission</span><span class="ku">joules (J)</span></div></div><p>About 31 billion fissions every second, for just one watt. A 3000 MW reactor needs about 10<sup>20</sup> every second.</p>`),
N(`How many fissions per second make 1 watt? Give the number in front of × 10<sup>10</sup>.`,3.125,0.03,'× 10¹⁰ per second',`1 ÷ 3.2 × 10<sup>−11</sup> = 0.3125 × 10<sup>11</sup> = 3.1 × 10<sup>10</sup>.`,`1 ÷ 3.2 = 0.3125, and dividing by 10<sup>−11</sup> is the same as multiplying by 10<sup>11</sup>.`),
Q(`Where does the energy released in fission come from?`,[`A small amount of mass turns into energy (E = mc²)`,'The kinetic energy of the incoming neutron','Chemical bonds breaking inside the fuel','The electrons dropping into lower energy levels'],0,`The pieces weigh 0.186 u less than what you started with. That missing mass became energy, through E = mc².`),
T(`<h3>Everything in one place</h3><table><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Means</th><th>Unit or value</th></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>energy</td><td>joules (J)</td></tr><tr><td>m</td><td>mass</td><td>kilograms (kg)</td></tr><tr><td>c</td><td>speed of light</td><td>3.00 × 10<sup>8</sup> m/s</td></tr><tr><td>c<sup>2</sup></td><td>c × c</td><td>9 × 10<sup>16</sup></td></tr><tr><td>u</td><td>atomic mass unit</td><td>1.66 × 10<sup>−27</sup> kg</td></tr><tr><td>eV</td><td>electronvolt</td><td>1.60 × 10<sup>−19</sup> J</td></tr><tr><td>MeV</td><td>a million eV</td><td>1.60 × 10<sup>−13</sup> J</td></tr></table><div class="callout"><b>Shortcut:</b> 1 u of mass = 931.5 MeV. <b>One fission</b> ≈ 200 MeV ≈ 3.2 × 10<sup>−11</sup> J.</div>`),
Q(`Fissioning 1 kg of uranium-235 releases about as much energy as burning…`,[`about 3000 tonnes of coal`,`1 tonne of coal`,`30 tonnes of coal`,`3 million tonnes of coal`],0,`1 kg of U-235 is about 2.6 × 10<sup>24</sup> atoms × 3.2 × 10<sup>−11</sup> J ≈ 8 × 10<sup>13</sup> J. Coal gives about 27 million J per kg, so that's around 3000 tonnes.`)])
]});

U({id:'nuclear',title:'Atomic and nuclear physics',icon:'²³⁵U',color:'#1D9E75',desc:'The nucleus, binding energy, decay, half-life, how neutrons interact, and fission.',lessons:[
L('a1','Inside the atom',8,[
T(`<h3>The nucleus</h3><p>An atom has a tiny nucleus of <b>protons</b> and <b>neutrons</b> (together called nucleons), surrounded by electrons. We write a nuclide like this:</p><div class="eq"><sup>235</sup><sub>92</sub>U</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">235 (top)</span><span class="km">mass number A: protons + neutrons</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">92 (bottom)</span><span class="km">atomic number Z: protons only (this decides the element)</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">U</span><span class="km">chemical symbol: uranium</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="kn">Neutrons = 235 − 92 = 143.</div></div><p>The top number (A) is the number of nucleons. The bottom number (Z) is the number of protons, which decides the element.</p>`),
Q(`How many neutrons are in <sup>235</sup><sub>92</sub>U?`,['92','143','235','327'],1,`Neutrons = A − Z = 235 − 92 = 143.`),
T(`<h3>Isotopes</h3><p>Isotopes are the same element with different numbers of neutrons. U-235 and U-238 behave identically in chemistry but very differently in a reactor: <b>U-235 fissions easily with slow neutrons; U-238 mostly doesn't</b>.</p><p>Natural uranium is only 0.72% U-235. PWR fuel is <b>enriched</b> to about 3–5% U-235.</p>`),
T(`<h3>What holds the nucleus together?</h3><p>Protons repel each other. The <b>strong nuclear force</b> holds nucleons together, but it only works over very short distances. Big nuclei need extra neutrons to add "glue" without adding repulsion. If the balance of neutrons and protons is wrong, the nucleus is unstable and decays.</p>`),
W('nuclideChart',{},`This is a chart of nuclides: protons up, neutrons across. Stable nuclei sit in the dark band. Fission fragments are medium-sized nuclei with <b>too many neutrons</b>. Click where they would be.`,`Fission fragments land below and to the right of the stable band: neutron-rich. They fix this by beta-minus decay, turning neutrons into protons, often several times in a row.`),
Q(`Fission fragments have too many neutrons. How do they usually decay?`,['Alpha decay','Beta-minus decay','Beta-plus decay','They never decay'],1,`Beta-minus decay turns a neutron into a proton, moving the nucleus towards the stable band. Each fragment usually goes through a chain of these decays.`)]),
L('a2','Binding energy',8,[
T(`<h3>The missing mass</h3><p>A nucleus weighs <b>less</b> than the protons and neutrons it's made of. The missing mass, the <b>mass defect</b>, was released as energy when the nucleus formed.</p><p>The <b>binding energy</b> is the energy needed to pull a nucleus completely apart. Divide it by the number of nucleons to compare nuclei fairly.</p>`),
W('beCurve',{},`Click the points on the curve. Find the <b>most tightly bound</b> nucleus.`,`Iron-56 (and nickel-62) sit at the top of the curve. Moving any nucleus towards the top releases energy.`),
T(`<h3>Why fission releases energy</h3><p>Uranium sits on the right of the curve at about 7.6 MeV per nucleon. Its fission fragments are higher, at about 8.5 MeV per nucleon. Splitting uranium moves nucleons up the curve, and the difference comes out as energy.</p>`),
N(`Estimate the energy released when 235 nucleons go from 7.6 to 8.5 MeV per nucleon.`,211.5,0.06,'MeV',`(8.5 − 7.6) × 235 ≈ 210 MeV. That matches the ~200 MeV per fission measured in reactors.`),
Q(`Why do both fission of uranium and fusion of hydrogen release energy?`,['Both join small nuclei together to make heavier nuclei','Both move nuclei towards the peak of the binding energy curve','Both are chemical reactions that break bonds between atoms','Both absorb neutrons, which adds energy to the nuclei'],1,`Fusion climbs the curve from the left, fission from the right. Either way the products are more tightly bound.`)]),
L('a3','Radioactive decay',10,[
T(`<h3>Three main types</h3><table><tr><th>Decay</th><th>What leaves</th><th>Change</th></tr><tr><td>Alpha α</td><td>helium nucleus (2p + 2n)</td><td>A − 4, Z − 2</td></tr><tr><td>Beta-minus β⁻</td><td>electron (and antineutrino)</td><td>Z + 1 (neutron → proton)</td></tr><tr><td>Gamma γ</td><td>high-energy photon</td><td>no change in A or Z</td></tr></table><p>There's also beta-plus β⁺ (a proton becomes a neutron, Z − 1).</p>`),
W('decayDrill',{},`Work out the daughter nucleus. Get <b>3 in a row</b> right.`,`Both numbers must balance on each side of a nuclear equation.`),
T(`<h3>Decay chains in a reactor</h3><p>Fission products decay through chains of beta-minus decays. One of the most important for operators:</p><div class="eq">I-135 → Xe-135 → Cs-135</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">I</span><span class="km">iodine</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">Xe</span><span class="km">xenon</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">Cs</span><span class="km">caesium</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">135</span><span class="km">mass number (protons + neutrons): it stays 135 because beta decay swaps a neutron for a proton</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">→</span><span class="km">“decays into”</span><span class="ku"></span></div></div><p>Xenon-135 absorbs neutrons extremely strongly. You'll meet it again in unit 4.</p>`),
Q(`I-135 (Z = 53) undergoes beta-minus decay. What does it become?`,['Xe-135 (Z = 54)','Te-135 (Z = 52)','I-134','Sb-131'],0,`Beta-minus: Z goes up by 1 and A stays the same. Element 54 is xenon.`),
Q(`Which is the most penetrating?`,['Alpha','Beta','Gamma'],2,`Gamma rays have no charge and no mass, so they interact rarely and need thick shielding like lead, steel or concrete.`)]),
L('a4','Half-life and activity',10,[
T(`<h3>Half-life</h3><p>Each nucleus has a fixed chance of decaying per second: the <b>decay constant λ</b>. In a big sample this gives:</p><div class="eq">N = N<sub>0</sub> e<sup>−λt</sup> &nbsp; and &nbsp; activity A = λN</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">N</span><span class="km">number of radioactive nuclei left</span><span class="ku">a count (no unit)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">N<sub>0</sub></span><span class="km">number you started with</span><span class="ku">a count</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">e</span><span class="km">a special number, about 2.718</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">λ</span><span class="km">“lambda”, the <b>decay constant</b>: the fraction that decays each second</span><span class="ku">per second (s<sup>−1</sup>)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">t</span><span class="km">time</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">A</span><span class="km"><b>activity</b>: how many nuclei decay each second</span><span class="ku">becquerels (Bq)</span></div><div class="kn">Here A is activity, not the mass number. Same letter, different meaning.</div></div><p>The <b>half-life</b> is the time for half to decay: <b>t<sub>½</sub> = ln 2 / λ = 0.693 / λ</b>.</p>`),
W('halfLife',{},`Read the half-life of this mystery isotope from the graph.`,`Start at any value, find where it has halved, and read the time. Checking twice (half again) makes your answer more reliable.`),
N(`Xenon-135 has a half-life of 9.1 hours. What fraction is left after 27.3 hours (with no new xenon being made)?`,0.125,0.01,'',`27.3 h is 3 half-lives: ½ × ½ × ½ = 1/8 = 0.125.`),
N(`Iodine-135 has a half-life of 6.6 hours. What is its decay constant?`,0.105,0.02,'per hour',`λ = 0.693 / 6.6 = 0.105 per hour.`),
Q(`A sample's activity is 800 Bq. After two half-lives it's…`,['400 Bq','200 Bq','100 Bq','0 Bq'],1,`800 → 400 → 200 Bq.`)]),
L('a5','How neutrons interact',12,[
T(`<h3>No charge, no repulsion</h3><p>Neutrons have no charge, so they can reach any nucleus easily. When a neutron meets a nucleus, it can:</p><ul><li><b>Scatter</b>: bounce off (elastic or inelastic)</li><li><b>Be captured</b>: absorbed, usually giving off a gamma ray (n,γ)</li><li><b>Cause fission</b>: in fissile nuclei like U-235</li><li><b>Cause another reaction</b>, like B-10 + n → Li-7 + α</li></ul>`),
Q(`Boron-10 absorbs a neutron and emits an alpha particle. What's this used for in a reactor?`,['Making more neutrons to speed the reaction up','Absorbing neutrons to control the reaction','Heating the coolant water with the alpha particles','Producing new fuel to keep the reactor going'],1,`Boron is a strong neutron absorber. It's dissolved in PWR coolant and used in control rods.`),
T(`<h3>Cross sections</h3><p>How likely an interaction is depends on the <b>microscopic cross section σ</b>: an effective target area. It's measured in <b>barns</b>: 1 barn = 10<sup>−24</sup> cm<sup>2</sup>.</p><p>Cross sections depend strongly on the neutron's energy. Slow neutrons often have much bigger cross sections.</p>`),
W('crossSection',{},`This is a simplified plot of cross sections against neutron energy. Find the energy of <b>U-238's biggest resonance</b>, where it absorbs neutrons very strongly.`,`U-238's biggest capture resonance is at about 6.7 eV. Neutrons slowing down through this energy can be captured. As fuel gets hotter, these resonances broaden: the Doppler effect, which you'll meet in unit 4.`),
T(`<h3>Why reactors slow neutrons down</h3><p>Neutrons are born fast (about 2 MeV). <b>Thermal</b> neutrons, slowed to about 0.025 eV (2200 m/s), are much more likely to cause fission in U-235:</p><table><tr><th>Neutron</th><th>U-235 fission cross section</th></tr><tr><td>Thermal (0.025 eV)</td><td>about 585 barns</td></tr><tr><td>Fast (about 1 MeV)</td><td>about 1.2 barns</td></tr></table>`),
N(`How many times bigger is U-235's fission cross section for thermal neutrons (585 b) than for fast neutrons (1.2 b)?`,487.5,0.03,'times',`585 ÷ 1.2 ≈ 490. That's why most power reactors use a moderator.`),
Q(`At low energies, U-235's cross section varies as 1/v. What happens when neutrons go slower?`,['The cross section gets smaller','The cross section gets bigger','No change','The neutron is destroyed'],1,`Slower neutrons spend longer near each nucleus, so they're more likely to interact.`)]),
L('a6','Neutron flux and reaction rates',10,[
T(`<h3>Macroscopic cross section</h3><p>A real material has N nuclei per cm<sup>3</sup>. Multiply by σ to get the <b>macroscopic cross section</b>:</p><div class="eq">Σ = N σ &nbsp;(per cm)</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">Σ</span><span class="km">“capital sigma”, <b>macroscopic cross-section</b>: chance of a hit per cm travelled</span><span class="ku">per cm (cm<sup>−1</sup>)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">N</span><span class="km">how many target nuclei in each cm³</span><span class="ku">nuclei per cm³</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">σ</span><span class="km">“sigma”, <b>microscopic cross-section</b>: the target size of one nucleus</span><span class="ku">cm² (or barns: 1 b = 10<sup>−24</sup> cm²)</span></div></div><p>Σ is the chance per cm of travel that a neutron interacts. The average distance between interactions, the <b>mean free path</b>, is 1/Σ.</p>`),
W('beamSlab',{},`A beam of neutrons hits a slab of absorber. Choose a thickness that stops <b>at least 99%</b> of the beam.`,`The beam falls exponentially: transmitted fraction = e<sup>−Σx</sup>. To get down to 1% you need Σx = ln 100 ≈ 4.6, about 4.6 mean free paths.`),
T(`<h3>Flux and reaction rate</h3><p><b>Neutron flux φ</b> is the number of neutrons crossing 1 cm<sup>2</sup> each second (n/cm<sup>2</sup>/s). In a PWR core, φ is roughly 10<sup>13</sup>–10<sup>14</sup>.</p><div class="eq">reaction rate R = φ Σ &nbsp;(per cm<sup>3</sup> per second)</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">R</span><span class="km">reactions happening in each cm³ every second</span><span class="ku">per cm³ per s</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">φ</span><span class="km">“phi”, <b>neutron flux</b>: how many neutrons pass through each cm² each second</span><span class="ku">neutrons per cm² per s</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">Σ</span><span class="km">macroscopic cross-section</span><span class="ku">per cm</span></div></div>`),
N(`φ = 3 × 10<sup>13</sup> n/cm²/s and Σ<sub>f</sub> = 0.1 per cm. How many fissions happen per cm³ each second? Give the number in front of ×10<sup>12</sup>.`,3,0.01,'× 10¹² per cm³ per s',`R = φΣ = 3 × 10<sup>13</sup> × 0.1 = 3 × 10<sup>12</sup> fissions per cm<sup>3</sup> per second.`),
N(`Each fission gives 3.2 × 10<sup>−11</sup> J. What power does that rate (3 × 10<sup>12</sup> per cm³ per s) produce in each cm³?`,96,0.02,'W per cm³',`3 × 10<sup>12</sup> × 3.2 × 10<sup>−11</sup> = 96 W in every cubic centimetre.`),
Q(`Why can neutron detectors outside the core measure reactor power?`,['They measure the core temperature directly','Power is proportional to the neutron flux','They count electrons given off by the fuel','They measure the steam flow to the turbine'],1,`Fission rate = φΣ<sub>f</sub>, so power is proportional to flux. Detectors near the core see a fixed fraction of the neutrons leaking out.`)]),
L('a7','Fission',12,[
T(`<h3>Splitting the nucleus</h3><p>A U-235 nucleus absorbs a neutron and becomes U-236 in an excited state. It wobbles like a drop of liquid and splits into two <b>fission fragments</b>, releasing <b>2 or 3 neutrons</b> and about <b>200 MeV</b>.</p>`),
W('fissionEnergy',{},`Click each part of the fission energy bar. Find the part that <b>escapes the reactor completely</b> and is never turned into heat.`,`Neutrinos interact so weakly they fly straight out of the reactor and the Earth. That's why we say about 200 MeV per fission is recoverable, out of about 207 MeV in total.`),
T(`<h3>Where the heat appears</h3><p>About 168 MeV is kinetic energy of the fragments. They stop within micrometres, inside the fuel pellet, so <b>the fuel gets hot first</b>. The heat then flows out through the cladding into the coolant.</p>`),
W('fissionYield',{},`Fission rarely splits a nucleus into two equal halves. Click on the curve to find a <b>likely fragment mass</b>.`,`The fragments are usually unequal, with mass numbers around 90–100 and 130–145. Examples: krypton and barium, or strontium and xenon.`),
T(`<h3>Prompt and delayed neutrons</h3><p>On average about 2.4 neutrons come out of each U-235 fission. Over 99% are <b>prompt</b>: released instantly. About 0.65% are <b>delayed</b>: released seconds to about a minute later by decaying fission products.</p><div class="callout">This tiny delayed fraction is what makes a reactor controllable. Unit 5 explains why.</div>`),
N(`Each fission releases about 2.4 neutrons. For steady power, how many of them must, on average, go on to cause another fission?`,1,0.001,'',`Exactly 1. The rest leak out or are absorbed. That balance is what "critical" means.`),
Q(`Why do fission products matter even after the chain reaction stops?`,['They keep fissioning, producing more neutrons','They keep decaying, giving off heat (decay heat)','They slowly turn back into uranium fuel','They absorb heat, so the core cools down fast'],1,`Fission products are radioactive. Their decay heat is about 6–7% of full power straight after shutdown. Removing it is vital.`)])
]});

U({id:'statics',title:'Reactor theory',icon:'k',color:'#D85A30',desc:'The chain reaction: k, reactivity, moderation, rods, boron, feedback, xenon and shutdown margin.',lessons:[
L('r1','The neutron life cycle',8,[
T(`<h3>Generation after generation</h3><p>Neutrons in a reactor live in <b>generations</b>. Each generation, some neutrons cause fission and make new neutrons. Others leak out of the core or get absorbed without causing fission.</p><div class="eq">k = neutrons in this generation ÷ neutrons in the last generation</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">k</span><span class="km">multiplication factor: 1 = critical (steady), above 1 = growing, below 1 = dying away</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div></div><p>k is the <b>effective multiplication factor</b>.</p>`),
W('generations',{},`Adjust k and watch the neutron population over 20 generations. Make the population <b>perfectly steady</b>.`,`When k = 1 exactly, each generation is the same size as the last: the reactor is critical.`),
Q(`What does k = 1 mean?`,['No neutrons are left, so the reactor is shut down','Each generation has exactly as many neutrons as the last: critical','Each generation has more neutrons than the last: power is rising','Each generation has fewer neutrons than the last: power is falling'],1,`k = 1 is critical. k < 1 is subcritical (power falling), k > 1 is supercritical (power rising).`),
T(`<h3>Critical isn't dangerous</h3><p>In everyday English "critical" sounds alarming. In a reactor it means <b>perfectly balanced</b>. A reactor running steadily at any power, from a tiny fraction of a percent to 100%, is exactly critical.</p>`),
Q(`A reactor is running steadily at 50% power. It is…`,['Subcritical','Critical','Supercritical'],1,`Steady power means k = 1, whatever the power level.`),
Q(`Power is slowly rising. k is…`,['Slightly less than 1','Exactly 1','Slightly more than 1','Exactly 0'],2,`Each generation is slightly bigger than the last, so k is just above 1.`)]),
L('r2','The six-factor formula',12,[
T(`<h3>Following a generation</h3><p>We can split k into six factors, each describing one stage of a neutron's life:</p><table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>What it describes</th></tr><tr><td>ε fast fission</td><td>extra neutrons from fast fission (mostly U-238)</td></tr><tr><td>L<sub>f</sub> fast non-leakage</td><td>fraction of fast neutrons that don't leak out</td></tr><tr><td>p resonance escape</td><td>fraction that slow down without being captured in U-238's resonances</td></tr><tr><td>L<sub>t</sub> thermal non-leakage</td><td>fraction of thermal neutrons that don't leak out</td></tr><tr><td>f thermal utilisation</td><td>fraction of thermal absorptions that happen in the fuel</td></tr><tr><td>η reproduction</td><td>new fast neutrons per thermal neutron absorbed in fuel</td></tr></table><div class="eq">k = ε · L<sub>f</sub> · p · L<sub>t</sub> · f · η</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">k</span><span class="km">neutrons in one generation ÷ neutrons in the one before. 1 = steady</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">ε</span><span class="km">“epsilon”, fast fission factor: extra neutrons from fast fissions</span><span class="ku">no unit (about 1.03)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">L<sub>f</sub></span><span class="km">chance a fast neutron <b>doesn’t</b> leak out of the core</span><span class="ku">no unit (0 to 1)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">p</span><span class="km">chance a neutron slows down without being captured on the way</span><span class="ku">no unit (0 to 1)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">L<sub>t</sub></span><span class="km">chance a slow neutron doesn’t leak out</span><span class="ku">no unit (0 to 1)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">f</span><span class="km">fraction of slow neutrons absorbed in the fuel (not elsewhere)</span><span class="ku">no unit (0 to 1)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">η</span><span class="km">“eta”, new neutrons made per neutron absorbed in the fuel</span><span class="ku">no unit (about 2)</span></div><div class="kn">The dots mean multiply.</div></div>`),
W('sixFactor',{},`Follow 1000 neutrons through one generation. Adjust the factors to make the reactor <b>critical (k = 1.000 ± 0.005)</b>.`,`Every way of changing k (rods, boron, temperature, fuel) works by changing one or more of these factors.`),
Q(`Adding boron to the coolant mainly reduces which factor?`,['ε (fast fission)','p (resonance escape)','f (thermal utilisation)','η (neutrons per absorption)'],2,`Boron absorbs thermal neutrons in the coolant, so fewer thermal absorptions happen in the fuel. That lowers f.`),
Q(`Adding a bit more moderator (water) mainly increases which factor?`,['ε (fast fission)','p (resonance escape)','η (neutrons per absorption)','L<sub>f</sub>'],1,`More moderator slows neutrons faster and keeps them away from the fuel while they pass through U-238's resonance energies, so p goes up. (Too much water starts to lower f, because water absorbs neutrons too.)`),
Q(`Which factor counts fissions caused by fast neutrons?`,['ε','p','f','L<sub>t</sub>'],0,`ε is the fast fission factor, usually about 1.03.`),
N(`ε = 1.03, L<sub>f</sub> = 0.97, p = 0.75, L<sub>t</sub> = 0.99, f = 0.73, η = 1.85. What is k?`,1.0018,0.003,'',`1.03 × 0.97 × 0.75 × 0.99 × 0.73 × 1.85 = 1.002: just supercritical.`)]),
L('r3','Moderation',12,[
T(`<h3>Slowing neutrons down</h3><p>Fission neutrons are born at about <b>2 MeV</b>. To cause fission well in U-235 they need slowing to about <b>0.025 eV</b>: almost 100 million times less energy.</p><p>A good moderator has light nuclei (to take lots of energy per collision), scatters neutrons often, and absorbs very few.</p>`),
W('thermalise',{},`Fire neutrons into different moderators and count how many collisions each one needs to reach thermal energy.`,null),
Q(`Which moderator needs the fewest collisions to slow a neutron down?`,['Graphite','Heavy water','Light water'],2,`Light water: about 20 collisions, because hydrogen is as heavy as a neutron. Heavy water needs about 35, graphite about 115.`),
Q(`Heavy water takes more collisions than light water. Why do CANDU reactors use it?`,['It\'s cheaper than light water, so the plant costs less','It absorbs far fewer neutrons, so natural uranium can be used','It boils at a lower temperature, so it makes steam more easily','It speeds neutrons up, so more of them cause fission'],1,`Hydrogen absorbs some neutrons; deuterium hardly any. The neutron economy is so good that CANDU reactors can run on unenriched uranium.`),
T(`<h3>The moderator-to-fuel ratio</h3><p>PWRs are deliberately <b>undermoderated</b>: there's slightly less water than would give the maximum k.</p><p>So if the water heats up and expands, there's less moderation and <b>k falls</b>. This is a key safety feature: overheating automatically reduces power.</p>`),
W('modRatio',{},`Heat the coolant and watch where the reactor sits on the curve.`,null),
Q(`In an undermoderated PWR core, the coolant temperature rises. What happens to k?`,['It rises','It falls','No change'],1,`Hotter water is less dense, so there's less moderation. In an undermoderated core that lowers k: a negative moderator temperature coefficient.`)]),
L('r4','Reactivity',10,[
T(`<h3>How far from critical?</h3><p>Operators rarely talk about k. They use <b>reactivity</b>, ρ (rho):</p><div class="eq">ρ = (k − 1) / k</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">ρ</span><span class="km">“rho”, <b>reactivity</b>: how far from exactly critical. 0 = critical, + = power rising, − = falling</span><span class="ku">no unit (often given in pcm)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">k</span><span class="km">the multiplication factor (neutrons now ÷ neutrons last generation)</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div></div><p>ρ = 0 means critical, positive means supercritical, negative means subcritical. It's measured in Δk/k, usually as <b>pcm</b> (10<sup>−5</sup> Δk/k).</p>`),
W('reactivityCalc',{target:100},`Set k to give a reactivity of <b>+100 pcm</b>.`,`k = 1.0010 gives ρ = 0.001/1.001 ≈ 100 pcm. Near k = 1, reactivity in pcm is about (k − 1) × 100,000.`),
N(`k = 0.98. What is ρ in pcm?`,-2041,0.01,'pcm',`ρ = (0.98 − 1)/0.98 = −0.0204 = −2041 pcm.`),
T(`<h3>Reactivity adds up</h3><p>The great thing about reactivity is that effects simply <b>add</b>. Pulling rods out might add +200 pcm, while hotter coolant adds −50 pcm. Operators keep a running <b>reactivity balance</b>.</p>`),
W('reactivityBalance',{},`Choose events that together keep the reactor <b>exactly critical</b> (total = 0 pcm). Pick at least two.`,`Operators do this constantly: when something adds reactivity, something else must take it away.`),
T(`<h3>Dollars</h3><p>Reactivity is sometimes given in <b>dollars ($)</b>: 1 $ equals the delayed neutron fraction β, about 650 pcm for U-235 fuel. You'll see why that matters in unit 5.</p>`),
Q(`Which reactor is supercritical?`,['ρ = −20 pcm','ρ = 0','ρ = +20 pcm','k = 0.999'],2,`Any positive reactivity is supercritical: power will rise.`)]),
L('r5','Subcritical multiplication',14,[
T(`<h3>A shut-down reactor isn't silent</h3><p>Even when shut down, there are always some neutrons: from a <b>neutron source</b> placed in the core, from spontaneous fission, and from gamma rays knocking neutrons out of nuclei.</p><p>With k below 1, each source neutron starts a chain that dies away. The closer k gets to 1, the longer each chain lasts.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Multiplication</h3><div class="eq">total neutrons per source neutron, M = 1 / (1 − k)</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">M</span><span class="km">subcritical multiplication: how many neutrons each source neutron ends up giving</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">k</span><span class="km">multiplication factor (below 1 here)</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="kn">As k gets close to 1, M shoots up.</div></div><p>So the count rate is proportional to 1/(1 − k). As k approaches 1, the count rate shoots up.</p>`),
N(`k = 0.95. Each source neutron leads to how many neutrons in total?`,20,0.01,'',`M = 1/(1 − 0.95) = 20.`),
T(`<h3>The 1/M plot</h3><p>During a startup, operators withdraw rods in steps. After each step they wait for the count rate to settle and plot <b>1/M = (first count rate) ÷ (new count rate)</b>.</p><p>1/M falls towards zero as k approaches 1. Drawing a line through the last two points and extending it to zero <b>predicts</b> where the reactor will go critical.</p>`),
W('approachCrit',{},`Withdraw the rods step by step, recording a 1/M point after each step. Predict the <b>critical rod position</b> to within 5%.`,`This is how every reactor startup approaches criticality: carefully, in steps, using predictions rather than guesswork.`),
N(`The count rate goes from 100 cps to 200 cps after a rod step. 1/M started at 1. What is it now?`,0.5,0.01,'',`1/M = 100/200 = 0.5.`),
Q(`As you get closer to critical, why does the count rate take longer to settle after each step?`,['The detector slows down as it gets closer to its limit','Each neutron chain lasts more generations, so it takes longer to build up','The control rods move more slowly near the top of the core','The neutron source gets weaker as it is used up, so each step adds fewer neutrons to count'],1,`Near k = 1 the chains are very long, so the population takes much longer to reach its new level. That's why patience matters during an approach to criticality.`)]),
L('r6','Control rods',10,[
T(`<h3>Absorbers you can move</h3><p>Control rods contain strong neutron absorbers such as boron carbide or a silver-indium-cadmium alloy. They're grouped into <b>banks</b>:</p><ul><li><b>Shutdown banks</b>: fully withdrawn at power, ready to shut the reactor down</li><li><b>Control banks</b>: moved to adjust reactivity</li></ul><p>On a trip, all rods drop into the core under gravity in about 2 seconds.</p>`),
W('rodWorth',{},`Move a control rod through the core. Find the position where moving it has the <b>biggest effect</b>.`,`Rod worth depends on the neutron flux where the rod tip is. Flux is highest in the middle of the core, so the middle few percent of travel are worth the most.`),
T(`<h3>Integral and differential worth</h3><p><b>Integral worth</b>: total reactivity from inserting the rod to a given depth (the S-shaped curve). <b>Differential worth</b>: reactivity per % of movement at that depth (the hump).</p>`),
N(`Differential rod worth is 8 pcm per % at mid-core. Moving the rods 5% adds how much reactivity?`,40,0.01,'pcm',`8 × 5 = 40 pcm.`),
Q(`Control rods are almost fully withdrawn (tips near the top of the core). Moving them in 5% has…`,['A large effect','A small effect','No effect at all','A positive effect'],1,`Near the top and bottom of the core the flux is low, so differential worth is small.`),
Q(`Shutdown margin calculations assume the single most reactive rod stays stuck out. Why?`,['It\'s a law of physics that one rod will always get stuck','To make sure the reactor still shuts down even if one rod fails to insert','Because that rod is known to be broken and can\'t move','To save time, because checking one stuck rod is quicker than working out the worth of every rod'],1,`It's a conservative assumption: the design must cope with a single failure.`)]),
L('r7','Soluble boron',10,[
T(`<h3>Boron in the water</h3><p>PWRs dissolve <b>boric acid</b> in the primary coolant. Boron-10 absorbs thermal neutrons strongly (about 3840 barns). Concentration is measured in <b>ppm</b> (parts per million).</p><p>Boron spreads evenly through the core and changes slowly, so it's used for slow, long-term changes: fuel burnup and xenon. Rods handle fast changes.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Boron worth</h3><p>A typical boron worth is about <b>−10 pcm per ppm</b> (it varies through the cycle). Adding boron is <b>boration</b>; removing it by adding pure water is <b>dilution</b>.</p>`),
N(`Boron worth is −10 pcm/ppm. To add +300 pcm, by how many ppm must the boron change? (Include the sign.)`,-30,0.01,'ppm',`+300 pcm ÷ −10 pcm/ppm = −30 ppm: dilute by 30 ppm.`),
W('dilution',{},`Dilute the coolant from <b>1200 ppm</b> to <b>1150 ppm</b> (±3 ppm) by choosing a pure-water flow and a time. The 300 m³ of coolant mixes as you go ("feed and bleed").`,`Concentration falls exponentially: C = C<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−Ft/V</sup>. Operators calculate the volume of water needed before starting.`),
Q(`Why don't PWRs use boron to trip the reactor?`,['Boron absorbs neutrons too slowly to stop the reaction','Changing boron takes minutes; rods drop in about 2 seconds','Boron is too expensive to dump into the coolant at once','Adding it quickly would damage the reactor coolant pumps'],1,`Boron is far too slow for a trip. (Emergency boration is still a backup way to shut down.)`),
Q(`Over an 18-month fuel cycle, the boron concentration at full power…`,['Rises as fission products build up and need balancing','Falls towards zero as the fuel is used up','Stays the same all the way through'],1,`Fresh fuel has lots of excess reactivity, held down by boron. As fuel burns, boron is diluted until it's nearly zero at the end of the cycle.`)]),
L('r8','Reactivity coefficients',14,[
T(`<h3>Feedback</h3><p>A <b>reactivity coefficient</b> is how much reactivity changes per unit change in something, for example pcm per °C. The important ones:</p><table><tr><th>Coefficient</th><th>Cause</th><th>PWR sign</th></tr><tr><td>Doppler (fuel temperature)</td><td>Hotter fuel broadens U-238 resonances, capturing more neutrons</td><td>Negative, acts in seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Moderator temperature (MTC)</td><td>Hotter water is less dense, so less moderation</td><td>Negative at power</td></tr><tr><td>Void</td><td>Steam bubbles replace water</td><td>Negative</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure</td><td>Higher pressure makes water slightly denser</td><td>Small, positive</td></tr></table>`),
W('selfReg',{},`Add a step of positive reactivity, choose the moderator temperature coefficient, and run. Make the reactor <b>settle at a new steady power on its own</b>.`,`With a negative coefficient, the temperature rise cancels the added reactivity and the reactor settles by itself. That's called inherent stability.`),
Q(`Power rises suddenly. Which feedback acts first?`,['Doppler (fuel temperature)','Moderator (water temperature)','Xenon (fission poison)','Boron (soluble poison)'],0,`The fuel heats up within seconds, before the heat has reached the coolant. Doppler feedback is prompt and always negative in low-enriched fuel.`),
N(`The MTC is −30 pcm/°C. The coolant cools by 4 °C. What's the reactivity change?`,120,0.01,'pcm',`−30 × (−4) = +120 pcm. Cooling adds reactivity, so power will rise.`),
Q(`Chernobyl's RBMK reactor had a positive void coefficient. What did that mean?`,['More steam made the reactor less reactive, so boiling shut it down','More steam made the reactor more reactive, so power and boiling could feed each other','Steam had no effect on reactivity, so only the control rods and the boron could change the power','The reactor couldn\'t boil, so there was never any steam in the core'],1,`More power → more boiling → more reactivity → more power. At low power this effect dominated, and it contributed to the 1986 accident.`),
T(`<h3>Load following</h3><p>A negative MTC lets a PWR follow the turbine. If the turbine takes more steam, the coolant cools, adding reactivity, so power rises to match. Raising power from 0 to 100% also heats everything up, adding roughly <b>−1500 to −2000 pcm</b> (the <b>power defect</b>), which operators compensate with rods and boron.</p>`),
Q(`The turbine takes more steam, so the coolant cools. With a negative MTC, the reactor…`,['Shuts down to protect the turbine','Increases power to match the demand','Decreases power to let the water warm','Stays at the same power the whole time'],1,`Cooler coolant adds reactivity, so power rises until the temperatures balance again.`)]),
L('r9','Xenon-135',14,[
T(`<h3>The most important poison</h3><p>Xenon-135 has an enormous absorption cross section: about <b>2.6 million barns</b>. Around 95% of it is made indirectly, through a decay chain:</p><div class="eq">Te-135 → I-135 (6.6 h) → Xe-135 (9.1 h) → Cs-135</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">Te</span><span class="km">tellurium</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">I</span><span class="km">iodine</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">Xe</span><span class="km">xenon</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">Cs</span><span class="km">caesium</span><span class="ku"></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">(6.6 h)</span><span class="km">half-life: time for half of it to decay</span><span class="ku">hours (h)</span></div></div><p>Xenon is removed in two ways: by decaying, and by <b>burnout</b> (absorbing a neutron to become Xe-136).</p>`),
O(`Put the xenon decay chain in order.`,['Te-135','I-135','Xe-135','Cs-135'],`Each step is a beta-minus decay. Iodine-135's 6.6 hour half-life is why xenon keeps arriving after a shutdown.`),
T(`<h3>Equilibrium</h3><p>At steady power, xenon builds up over about <b>40 to 50 hours</b> until production balances removal. At full power, equilibrium xenon is worth about <b>−2500 to −3000 pcm</b>.</p>`),
W('xenonSim',{},`Choose a scenario and move the time cursor. For a <b>trip from full power</b>, find when the xenon peaks.`,`After a trip, burnout stops instantly, but iodine keeps decaying into xenon for hours. Xenon peaks about 9–11 hours later, then decays away over a day or two.`),
Q(`Why does xenon increase after a trip?`,['Fission carries on after the trip and keeps making it','Burnout stops, but iodine already in the core keeps decaying into xenon','The control rods release xenon as they drop into the core','Boron in the coolant turns into xenon when it absorbs neutrons'],1,`Production (from iodine) carries on for hours; the biggest removal route (burnout) has stopped.`),
T(`<h3>Xenon dead time</h3><p>Near the end of a fuel cycle there's little excess reactivity left. If the xenon peak needs more reactivity than is available, the reactor <b>can't be restarted</b> until the xenon decays: often a day or more. You can try this in the control room (mission 4).</p><p>Large cores can also have <b>xenon oscillations</b>: power slowly sloshes between top and bottom of the core over about a day, and operators correct it with rods.</p>`),
N(`Xenon worth is −6000 pcm at its peak. If it simply decayed with a 9.1 h half-life (no new xenon), what would it be 18.2 h later?`,-1500,0.01,'pcm',`18.2 h is two half-lives: −6000 → −3000 → −1500 pcm.`)]),
L('r10','Samarium and burnable poisons',8,[
T(`<h3>Samarium-149</h3><p>Samarium-149 is the second most important fission product poison (about 41,000 barns). It's made from promethium-149 (half-life 53 hours) and is <b>stable</b>: it never decays. It's removed only by burnout.</p><ul><li>Its equilibrium level is the same at any steady power.</li><li>After shutdown, Pm-149 keeps decaying into it, so it <b>rises and then stays</b> until the reactor runs again.</li></ul>`),
Q(`After a shutdown, samarium-149…`,['Peaks then decays away like xenon','Rises to a new level and stays there','Disappears immediately','Turns into xenon'],1,`Samarium is stable, so with no burnout it just accumulates as promethium decays.`),
T(`<h3>Burnable poisons</h3><p>A fresh core has lots of excess reactivity. As well as boron, designers add <b>burnable poisons</b>, such as gadolinium mixed into some fuel pellets. They absorb neutrons early in life and burn away as the fuel is used.</p>`),
Q(`Why use burnable poisons rather than just more boron?`,['They\'re much cheaper than boron, and they don\'t need replacing between cycles, so they save money','High boron makes the moderator temperature coefficient less negative (or even positive), so burnable poisons reduce the boron needed','Boron becomes radioactive in the coolant, so using less of it makes the plant safer to work in and cheaper to clean up after each outage','They make more neutrons as they burn, which lets the fuel last longer without extra boron'],1,`When hot water expands, it also pushes boron out of the core. With very high boron, that can outweigh the moderation effect and make the MTC positive, which isn't allowed at power.`),
Q(`Other fission products build up through the cycle too. What does that do to the core's reactivity?`,['Increases it','Reduces it','No effect'],1,`They're all mild neutron absorbers, so they slowly reduce reactivity as the core ages.`)]),
L('r11','Core life and shutdown margin',12,[
T(`<h3>A fuel cycle</h3><p>A fresh PWR core has thousands of pcm of <b>excess reactivity</b>. Over about 18 months:</p><ul><li>U-235 is used up</li><li>Plutonium-239 builds up from U-238 and makes a growing share of the power</li><li>Fission product poisons accumulate</li></ul><p>Boron is gradually diluted to keep the reactor critical. When boron reaches about zero, the cycle ends and the core is refuelled.</p>`),
W('reactivityBudget',{},`Move through the fuel cycle and watch the reactivity budget. Find the <b>end of the cycle</b>, where boron reaches zero.`,`At end of cycle there's no boron left to dilute. Some plants can then run on a little longer by letting temperature and power drop ("coastdown").`),
T(`<h3>Shutdown margin</h3><p><b>Shutdown margin</b> is how far subcritical the reactor would be if all rods were inserted, assuming the most reactive rod stays stuck out. It must be kept above a minimum at all times.</p>`),
N(`All rods together are worth 7000 pcm. The most reactive rod is worth 1200 pcm. The core has +4500 pcm of excess reactivity to overcome. What's the shutdown margin?`,1300,0.01,'pcm',`Available: 7000 − 1200 = 5800 pcm. Shutdown margin = 5800 − 4500 = 1300 pcm.`),
Q(`Plutonium-239 has a delayed neutron fraction of 0.21%, compared with 0.65% for U-235. As plutonium builds up, the reactor…`,['Responds more slowly to reactivity','Responds faster to the same reactivity','Can\'t go critical','Doesn\'t change'],1,`Fewer delayed neutrons means shorter periods for the same reactivity, and prompt critical is reached sooner. Unit 5 explains why delayed neutrons matter so much.`)])
]});

U({id:'kinetics',title:'Reactor kinetics',icon:'τ',color:'#D4537E',desc:'Delayed neutrons, reactor period, startup rate, prompt criticality, trips and decay heat.',lessons:[
L('k1','Prompt and delayed neutrons',12,[
T(`<h3>A thought experiment</h3><p>In a PWR, a prompt neutron generation lasts about <b>2 × 10<sup>−5</sup> s</b>. If every neutron were prompt, the reactor period would be:</p><div class="eq">τ = ℓ / (k − 1)</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">τ</span><span class="km">reactor period: time for power to grow by a factor of 2.718</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">ℓ</span><span class="km">neutron lifetime: time for one generation</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">k</span><span class="km">multiplication factor</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div></div>`),
N(`If only prompt neutrons existed: ℓ = 2 × 10<sup>−5</sup> s and k = 1.001. What would the period τ be?`,0.02,0.01,'s',`τ = 2 × 10<sup>−5</sup> ÷ 0.001 = 0.02 s. Power would multiply by e fifty times a second. Nobody could control that.`),
T(`<h3>Delayed neutrons save the day</h3><p>About <b>0.65%</b> of U-235's fission neutrons (the <b>delayed neutron fraction, β</b>) come from decaying fission products, called <b>precursors</b>, after an average delay of about 12.7 s.</p><p>They make the <b>average</b> generation time about 0.08 s instead of 0.00002 s: about 4000 times longer.</p>`),
W('delayedVsPrompt',{},`Add +100 pcm and compare what happens with and without delayed neutrons.`,null),
Q(`Why can operators control a reactor at all?`,['Control rods move faster than any change in power','Delayed neutrons slow the response to minutes rather than milliseconds','The reactor is always subcritical, so power can\'t rise','Computers react in microseconds, much faster than any operator'],1,`As long as reactivity is less than β, the chain reaction depends on delayed neutrons, and power changes slowly enough to control.`),
T(`<h3>Six groups</h3><p>Delayed neutron precursors are grouped by half-life:</p><table><tr><th>Group</th><th>Half-life</th><th>Share of β</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>55.7 s</td><td>3%</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>22.7 s</td><td>22%</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>6.2 s</td><td>20%</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.3 s</td><td>40%</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.6 s</td><td>12%</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0.2 s</td><td>4%</td></tr></table>`),
Q(`The longest-lived group has a half-life of about 55 s. What does that limit?`,['How fast power can rise during a startup','How fast power can fall after a trip','The highest temperature the fuel can reach','How much boron can be added after a trip'],1,`After a trip, neutrons from this group keep arriving, so power can't fall faster than about an 80 s period (55 s ÷ 0.693).`)]),
L('k2','Period and startup rate',12,[
T(`<h3>Two ways to describe the rate</h3><p>The <b>reactor period τ</b> is the time for power to change by a factor of e. Control room instruments show the <b>startup rate (SUR)</b> in <b>decades per minute (DPM)</b>:</p><div class="eq">SUR = 26.06 / τ</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">SUR</span><span class="km"><b>startup rate</b>: how many times power grows tenfold per minute</span><span class="ku">decades per minute (DPM)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">τ</span><span class="km">reactor period</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">26.06</span><span class="km">a fixed number that converts period into DPM (60 ÷ 2.303)</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div></div><p>(26.06 = 60 ÷ ln 10.) 1 DPM means power multiplies by 10 every minute.</p>`),
N(`The period is 60 s. What's the startup rate?`,0.434,0.02,'DPM',`26.06 ÷ 60 = 0.43 DPM.`),
N(`The startup rate is 1 DPM. What's the period?`,26.06,0.02,'s',`τ = 26.06 ÷ 1 = 26 s.`),
T(`<h3>Linking period to reactivity</h3><p>For small positive reactivity, a handy approximation is:</p><div class="eq">τ ≈ (β − ρ) / (λ<sub>eff</sub> ρ)</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">τ</span><span class="km">reactor period</span><span class="ku">seconds (s)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">β</span><span class="km">delayed neutron fraction (about 0.0065)</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">ρ</span><span class="km">reactivity</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">λ<sub>eff</sub></span><span class="km">an average decay constant for the delayed-neutron atoms</span><span class="ku">per second (s<sup>−1</sup>)</span></div></div><p>with λ<sub>eff</sub> ≈ 0.08 s<sup>−1</sup>. More reactivity gives a shorter period.</p>`),
W('periodRho',{},`Find the reactivity that gives a startup rate of <b>1 DPM</b>.`,`About 210 pcm, roughly a third of β. Startups typically keep the rate below about 1 DPM.`),
N(`Power is at 10<sup>−6</sup>% and the startup rate is 0.5 DPM. How long to reach 10<sup>−1</sup>%?`,10,0.01,'minutes',`That's 5 decades at 0.5 decades per minute: 10 minutes.`),
Q(`A typical startup rate during a controlled reactor startup is…`,['About 0.5 to 1 DPM','About 10 to 20 DPM','About 50 to 100 DPM','Exactly 0 DPM (flat)'],0,`Startup rates are kept low so operators have plenty of time to respond. High startup rates trigger alarms and, in many designs, a reactor trip.`)]),
L('k3','Prompt jump and prompt criticality',12,[
T(`<h3>The prompt jump</h3><p>When reactivity changes suddenly, prompt neutrons respond almost instantly. Power jumps by a factor before settling into its stable period:</p><div class="eq">P<sub>1</sub>/P<sub>0</sub> ≈ β / (β − ρ)</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">P<sub>0</sub></span><span class="km">power just before the change</span><span class="ku">W or %</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">P<sub>1</sub></span><span class="km">power just after the quick “prompt jump”</span><span class="ku">same unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">β</span><span class="km">delayed neutron fraction</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">ρ</span><span class="km">reactivity added</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div></div>`),
N(`ρ = +100 pcm and β = 650 pcm. What's the prompt jump, P<sub>1</sub>/P<sub>0</sub>?`,1.18,0.01,'',`650 ÷ (650 − 100) = 1.18: an 18% jump, then a steady rise.`),
T(`<h3>Prompt critical</h3><p>If reactivity reaches β (1 dollar), the reactor is critical on <b>prompt neutrons alone</b>. It no longer waits for delayed neutrons, and the period drops to milliseconds. Only the fuel heating up (Doppler) can stop it.</p><div class="warnbox"><b>A reactor must never be allowed to go prompt critical.</b> Chernobyl reactor 4 did in 1986.</div>`),
W('dollarMeter',{},`Increase the reactivity and watch the period. Find where the period suddenly drops to <b>milliseconds</b>.`,`At ρ = β (1 $), the period collapses from minutes to milliseconds. The delayed neutrons no longer control the reactor.`),
Q(`Which is prompt critical for U-235 fuel (β = 650 pcm)?`,['ρ = 100 pcm','ρ = 400 pcm','ρ = 650 pcm','k = 1'],2,`ρ = β = 650 pcm = 1 dollar.`),
Q(`As plutonium builds up, β falls to about 500 pcm. Prompt critical is then reached with…`,['More reactivity','Less reactivity','The same reactivity'],1,`Prompt critical happens at ρ = β, so a smaller β means less reactivity is needed. That's one reason end-of-cycle cores respond faster.`)]),
L('k4','After a reactor trip',10,[
T(`<h3>What a trip does</h3><p>On a trip, the rods drop by gravity in about 2 seconds, adding several thousand pcm of negative reactivity. Power then falls in two stages:</p><ul><li><b>Prompt drop</b>: almost instantly, to about β/(β − ρ) of its starting value</li><li><b>Steady decline</b>: the remaining delayed neutrons die away, settling on a <b>−80 s period</b></li></ul>`),
N(`A trip adds −6500 pcm, and β = 650 pcm. Using P<sub>1</sub>/P<sub>0</sub> = β/(β − ρ), what fraction of the original power is left after the prompt drop? Give a percentage.`,9.09,0.02,'%',`650 ÷ (650 + 6500) = 0.091, about 9%.`),
W('tripPlot',{},`Here's fission power after a trip from 100% (log scale). Move the cursor to find when power has fallen to <b>0.01%</b>.`,`Power falls almost instantly to about 9%, then on a slow −80 s period.`),
N(`After the prompt drop to 9%, power falls on a −80 s period. Roughly how long until it reaches 0.01%?`,545,0.06,'s',`t = 80 × ln(9 ÷ 0.01) = 80 × 6.8 ≈ 545 s, about 9 minutes.`),
Q(`Why can't fission power fall faster than about an 80 s period after a trip?`,['The control rods take about 80 seconds to fall all the way to the bottom of the core','The longest-lived precursors (55 s half-life) keep releasing delayed neutrons','The detectors take that long to respond to a change','Xenon builds up and stops the power falling any faster'],1,`55 s ÷ ln 2 = 80 s. Those last precursors set the pace.`)]),
L('k5','Decay heat',10,[
T(`<h3>Heat that won't switch off</h3><p>When fission stops, fission products keep decaying. Their <b>decay heat</b> is about:</p><table><tr><th>Time after trip</th><th>Decay heat (% of full power)</th></tr><tr><td>A few seconds</td><td>about 6%</td></tr><tr><td>1 hour</td><td>about 1–1.5%</td></tr><tr><td>1 day</td><td>about 0.5%</td></tr></table><p>For a 3000 MWth core, even 1% is 30 MW: enough to boil water away quickly if cooling stops.</p>`),
W('decayHeat',{},`This uses the Way–Wigner approximation. Move the cursor to find when decay heat falls below <b>1% of full power</b>.`,`Within a few hours decay heat drops to around 1%, but it takes days to fall to a few tenths of a percent. Cooling must continue for a long time.`),
N(`A 3000 MWth reactor has decay heat of 1.5% an hour after a trip. How many MW is that?`,45,0.01,'MW',`3000 × 0.015 = 45 MW.`),
N(`30 MW of decay heat is boiling water that's already at 100 °C. Latent heat = 2.26 MJ/kg. How many kg of water boil away every second?`,13.3,0.02,'kg/s',`30 ÷ 2.26 = 13.3 kg every second, about 1100 tonnes a day.`),
Q(`At Fukushima Daiichi in 2011, the reactors shut down successfully when the earthquake struck. Why did the cores still melt?`,['The control rods didn\'t insert, so the chain reaction kept going','The tsunami knocked out power to the cooling systems, so decay heat couldn\'t be removed','Xenon built up after the trip and exploded inside the core','The earthquake shook the reactors so hard that they went prompt critical as the rods dropped in'],1,`Shutting down the chain reaction is only half the job. Decay heat must be removed for days afterwards.`)])
]});

U({id:'thermo',title:'Thermodynamics',icon:'°C',color:'#BA7517',desc:'Heat, pressure, boiling and saturation, energy balances and the steam cycle.',lessons:[
L('t1','Temperature, pressure and heat',8,[
T(`<h3>Units you'll see on every panel</h3><ul><li><b>Temperature</b>: °C, or kelvin for physics (K = °C + 273.15)</li><li><b>Pressure</b>: bar (1 bar = 10<sup>5</sup> Pa, about atmospheric) or MPa (1 MPa = 10 bar)</li><li><b>Absolute</b> pressure counts from vacuum; <b>gauge</b> pressure counts from atmospheric</li></ul>`),
N(`PWR primary pressure is about 155 bar. What's that in MPa?`,15.5,0.01,'MPa',`1 MPa = 10 bar, so 155 bar = 15.5 MPa.`),
Q(`A gauge reads 154 bar(g). What's the absolute pressure, roughly?`,['154 bar(a)','155 bar(a)','153 bar(a)','1 bar(a)'],1,`Absolute = gauge + atmospheric ≈ 154 + 1 = 155 bar(a).`),
T(`<h3>Heating water</h3><div class="eq">Q = m c ΔT</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">Q</span><span class="km">heat energy put in</span><span class="ku">joules (J)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">m</span><span class="km">mass being heated</span><span class="ku">kilograms (kg)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">c</span><span class="km"><b>specific heat capacity</b>: energy to heat 1 kg by 1 °C</span><span class="ku">J/(kg·°C)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">ΔT</span><span class="km">temperature rise</span><span class="ku">°C</span></div><div class="kn">Careful: this <b>c</b> is <i>not</i> the speed of light. Same letter, different meaning.</div></div><p>c is the specific heat capacity. For water at room temperature, c = 4.18 kJ/kg/K. At PWR conditions (about 300 °C, 155 bar) it's higher, about 5.5 kJ/kg/K.</p>`),
N(`How much energy heats 1000 kg of water by 10 °C (c = 4.18 kJ/kg/K)?`,41.8,0.01,'MJ',`1000 × 4.18 × 10 = 41,800 kJ = 41.8 MJ.`),
N(`During a heat-up after an outage, the reactor coolant pumps add about 20 MW of heat. The primary circuit (water and steel) has a heat capacity of about 1500 MJ/°C. Roughly how fast does it heat up, in °C per hour?`,48,0.03,'°C per hour',`20 MJ/s ÷ 1500 MJ/°C = 0.0133 °C/s × 3600 = 48 °C per hour. Heat-up rate limits are typically about 55 °C per hour (see unit 9).`)]),
L('t2','Boiling and saturation',12,[
T(`<h3>Boiling depends on pressure</h3><p>Water boils at 100 °C only at atmospheric pressure. The boiling point at any pressure is the <b>saturation temperature</b>, T<sub>sat</sub>.</p><table><tr><th>Pressure</th><th>T<sub>sat</sub></th></tr><tr><td>1 bar</td><td>100 °C</td></tr><tr><td>70 bar (typical steam generator)</td><td>about 286 °C</td></tr><tr><td>155 bar (PWR primary)</td><td>about 345 °C</td></tr></table><p>That's why a PWR is pressurised: so its water stays liquid at about 325 °C.</p>`),
W('satCurve',{},`Move the pressure slider to find the pressure at which water boils at <b>300 °C</b>.`,`About 86 bar. Pressure and saturation temperature always go together: control one and you've set the other.`),
T(`<h3>Subcooling margin</h3><p>Operators continuously watch the <b>subcooling margin</b>:</p><div class="eq">subcooling margin = T<sub>sat</sub> − T<sub>hot leg</sub></div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">T<sub>sat</sub></span><span class="km">temperature at which water would boil at the current pressure</span><span class="ku">°C</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">T<sub>hot leg</sub></span><span class="km">temperature of the hottest coolant leaving the core</span><span class="ku">°C</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">margin</span><span class="km">how many degrees below boiling you are</span><span class="ku">°C</span></div></div><p>It shows how far the hottest coolant is from boiling. If it drops to zero, the water can boil in the core.</p>`),
N(`Primary pressure is 155 bar (T<sub>sat</sub> 345 °C) and the hot leg is at 325 °C. What's the subcooling margin?`,20,0.01,'°C',`345 − 325 = 20 °C.`),
W('subcool',{},`The hot leg is at 325 °C. Lower the pressure as far as you can while keeping at least <b>15 °C</b> of subcooling margin.`,`About 146 bar. Lose pressure (a stuck-open valve, say) and the margin disappears quickly, even at constant temperature.`),
Q(`If primary pressure falls but temperature stays the same, the subcooling margin…`,['Increases','Decreases','Doesn\'t change'],1,`Lower pressure means a lower saturation temperature, so the water is closer to boiling.`)]),
L('t3','Energy balance',10,[
T(`<h3>Heat carried by flowing water</h3><p>For water flowing through the core:</p><div class="eq">P = ṁ c ΔT</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">P</span><span class="km">heat carried away each second (power)</span><span class="ku">watts (W)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">ṁ</span><span class="km">mass flow rate: kilograms of water passing each second (the dot means “per second”)</span><span class="ku">kg/s</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">c</span><span class="km"><b>specific heat capacity</b>: energy to heat 1 kg by 1 °C</span><span class="ku">J/(kg·°C)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">ΔT</span><span class="km">“delta T”, the temperature rise (Δ means “change in”)</span><span class="ku">°C</span></div><div class="kn">Careful: this <b>c</b> is <i>not</i> the speed of light. Same letter, different meaning.</div></div><p>ṁ is the mass flow rate (kg/s) and ΔT is the temperature rise from inlet (cold leg) to outlet (hot leg).</p>`),
W('heatBalance',{},`Remove <b>3000 MW</b> from the core with a temperature rise of <b>33 °C</b> (c = 5.5 kJ/kg/K). Find the flow needed.`,`About 16,500 kg/s, more than 16 tonnes of water every second, pumped by the reactor coolant pumps.`),
Q(`At the same power, coolant flow halves. What happens to the temperature rise across the core?`,['It halves','It doubles','It stays the same','It quadruples'],1,`P = ṁcΔT: if ṁ halves at the same P, ΔT doubles. That's why loss of flow trips the reactor.`),
T(`<h3>Calibrating the power instruments</h3><p>Neutron detectors show power, but they drift. Operators regularly do a <b>calorimetric</b> (heat balance) on the steam generators: feedwater flow, temperatures and steam pressure give the true thermal power. The nuclear instruments are then adjusted to match.</p>`),
Q(`The power range instruments read 98%, but the calorimetric says 100%. What's done?`,['Nothing: the instruments are trusted over the calorimetric','The nuclear instruments are adjusted to read 100%','The reactor is tripped until the difference is explained','Power is reduced until the calorimetric also reads 98%'],1,`The calorimetric is the reference for true thermal power.`)]),
L('t4','Steam and the Rankine cycle',10,[
T(`<h3>The steam cycle</h3><p>The secondary side of a PWR runs a <b>Rankine cycle</b>: water is pumped to the steam generators, boiled, expanded through the turbine, condensed back to water, and pumped round again.</p>`),
O(`Put the stages of the steam cycle in order, starting at the feed pumps.`,['Feed pumps push water to the steam generators','Water boils in the steam generators','Steam expands through the turbine','Steam condenses back to water in the condenser'],`Then the cycle repeats. The condenser is where most of the heat leaves the plant.`),
T(`<h3>Why not 100% efficient?</h3><p>A heat engine must reject some heat to a cold reservoir. The best possible efficiency (the Carnot limit) depends only on the hot and cold temperatures, <b>in kelvin</b>:</p><div class="eq">η<sub>max</sub> = 1 − T<sub>cold</sub> / T<sub>hot</sub></div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">η<sub>max</sub></span><span class="km">“eta max”, the best possible efficiency</span><span class="ku">no unit (× 100 for %)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">T<sub>hot</sub></span><span class="km">temperature of the steam</span><span class="ku"><b>kelvin (K)</b></span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">T<sub>cold</sub></span><span class="km">temperature of the condenser</span><span class="ku"><b>kelvin (K)</b></span></div><div class="kn">Temperatures <b>must</b> be in kelvin: K = °C + 273. In °C the answer is wrong.</div></div>`),
W('carnot',{},`Explore how the steam and condenser temperatures limit efficiency.`,null),
N(`Steam at 285 °C, condenser at 25 °C. What's the Carnot limit?`,46.6,0.02,'%',`1 − 298/558 = 0.466. Real PWR plants reach about 35%.`),
Q(`Why does a good condenser vacuum matter?`,['It cools the reactor directly, so the fuel stays well below its limits','A lower condenser temperature means more energy can be extracted, so efficiency rises','It stops corrosion in the turbine, so the blades last much longer','It raises the steam pressure in the condenser, so the turbine can push harder and do more work'],1,`Lower T<sub>cold</sub> raises the efficiency limit. Losing condenser vacuum reduces output and can trip the turbine.`)])
]});

U({id:'heat',title:'Heat transfer and fluid flow',icon:'q″',color:'#1f8fb3',desc:'How heat gets out of the fuel, boiling and DNB, pumps, cavitation and natural circulation.',lessons:[
L('h1','Conduction and convection',12,[
T(`<h3>Getting heat out of the fuel</h3><p>Heat made in a fuel pellet must cross: the pellet itself, a small gas gap, the zirconium alloy cladding, and a thin film of water at the surface.</p><ul><li><b>Conduction</b> through solids: q = k A ΔT / thickness</li><li><b>Convection</b> into moving water: q = h A (T<sub>surface</sub> − T<sub>water</sub>)</li></ul>`),
W('fuelPin',{},`Increase the <b>linear heat rate</b> (power per metre of fuel rod). Find the highest value that keeps the pellet centre <b>below 2000 °C</b>.`,`About 46 kW/m. Uranium dioxide melts at about 2800 °C, so designs keep a big margin. Average PWR rods run at about 18 kW/m.`),
Q(`Which part of a fuel rod has the biggest temperature drop?`,['The thin film of water','The metal cladding tube','The helium gas gap','The fuel pellet itself'],3,`Uranium dioxide is a poor conductor (k ≈ 3 W/m/K), so the pellet centre runs hundreds of degrees hotter than its surface.`),
N(`For a cylindrical pellet, centre − surface temperature = q′/(4πk). If q′ = 20,000 W/m and k = 3 W/m/K, what's the temperature difference?`,530.5,0.01,'°C',`20,000 ÷ (4π × 3) = 531 °C.`),
Q(`Why does the cladding temperature matter so much in an accident?`,['Above about 1200 °C, zirconium reacts with steam, making hydrogen and weakening the cladding','The cladding melts at about 300 °C, so fuel would fall out of the rods','Hot cladding absorbs more neutrons, so the reactor would shut itself down','It doesn\'t matter much: the fuel pellet temperature is the only limit that operators need to watch'],0,`The zirconium–steam reaction made the hydrogen that exploded at Fukushima. Safety limits keep peak cladding temperature below 1204 °C in an accident.`)]),
L('h2','Boiling heat transfer and DNB',12,[
T(`<h3>The boiling curve</h3><p>As a surface gets hotter than the water's boiling point:</p><ul><li><b>Convection</b> only, at first</li><li><b>Nucleate boiling</b>: bubbles form and carry heat away very efficiently</li><li><b>Departure from nucleate boiling (DNB)</b>: bubbles merge into a vapour blanket</li><li><b>Film boiling</b>: steam insulates the surface, and its temperature shoots up</li></ul>`),
W('boilingCurve',{},`Slowly increase the heat flux from a fuel rod surface. Find out what happens at the <b>critical heat flux</b>.`,`Beyond the critical heat flux, the surface is blanketed in steam and its temperature jumps by hundreds of degrees at the same heat flux. Cladding could fail.`),
T(`<h3>DNB ratio</h3><div class="eq">DNBR = critical heat flux ÷ actual heat flux</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">DNBR</span><span class="km">departure from nucleate boiling ratio: safety margin before the fuel surface dries out</span><span class="ku">no unit (it must stay well above 1)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">heat flux</span><span class="km">heat crossing each square metre of fuel surface per second</span><span class="ku">W/m²</span></div></div><p>DNBR must stay above a design limit (around 1.3, depending on the design). Reactor trips on high power, low pressure, high temperature and low flow all help protect it.</p>`),
N(`The critical heat flux is 2.6 MW/m² and the actual heat flux is 1.6 MW/m². What's the DNBR?`,1.625,0.01,'',`2.6 ÷ 1.6 = 1.63.`),
Q(`Which change reduces DNBR (makes things worse)?`,['Higher pressure','Lower coolant flow','Lower power','Lower inlet temperature'],1,`Less flow removes heat less effectively and lowers the critical heat flux.`)]),
L('h3','Flow, pumps and cavitation',10,[
T(`<h3>Continuity and Bernoulli</h3><div class="eq">ṁ = ρ A v</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">ṁ</span><span class="km">mass flow rate</span><span class="ku">kg/s</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">ρ</span><span class="km"><b>density</b>: mass of 1 m³ of water</span><span class="ku">kg/m³</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">A</span><span class="km">cross-sectional area of the pipe</span><span class="ku">m²</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">v</span><span class="km">speed of the water</span><span class="ku">m/s</span></div><div class="kn">Careful: here ρ means <b>density</b>, not reactivity. Same letter, different meaning.</div></div><p>The same mass flow must pass every point in a pipe. Where the pipe narrows, the water speeds up. <b>Bernoulli</b>: where it speeds up, its pressure drops.</p>`),
Q(`A pipe's cross-sectional area halves. The water velocity…`,['Halves','Doubles','Stays the same','Quadruples'],1,`ṁ = ρAv: halve A and v must double.`),
W('venturi',{},`Narrow the pipe. Find where the pressure drops low enough for the water to start boiling: <b>cavitation</b>.`,`When local pressure falls below the vapour pressure, bubbles form and then collapse violently, damaging pump impellers and valves.`),
T(`<h3>Pumps and NPSH</h3><p>Pump inlets are low-pressure spots. A pump needs enough <b>net positive suction head (NPSH)</b>: enough pressure at its inlet above the vapour pressure. Too little and it cavitates: noise, vibration, damage and loss of flow.</p>`),
Q(`At Three Mile Island in 1979, operators stopped the reactor coolant pumps because they were vibrating badly. What was the likely cause?`,['Too much boron in the coolant, making the water too thick to pump','Steam bubbles in the coolant: the pumps were cavitating','Too much oil in the bearings, slowing the pumps','Electrical faults in the motors after the trip'],1,`Pressure had fallen and steam was forming in the primary circuit. Stopping the pumps then left the core with too little cooling.`)]),
L('h4','Natural circulation',8,[
T(`<h3>Flow without pumps</h3><p>Hot water is less dense than cold water. If the heat source (core) is <b>below</b> the heat sink (steam generators), hot water rises from the core and cool water sinks back down: <b>natural circulation</b>.</p><p>The driving force grows with the density difference and the height difference.</p>`),
W('natCirc',{},`Change the height between core and steam generators, and the decay heat, and watch the flow.`,null),
Q(`Why are PWR steam generators placed well above the core?`,['So the building takes up less space','So natural circulation can remove decay heat if the pumps stop','To keep them away from the hot core','So the steam generators shield the turbine hall from the core\'s radiation'],1,`If all the reactor coolant pumps stop, natural circulation can still carry decay heat to the steam generators, as long as they're supplied with feedwater.`),
Q(`Natural circulation flow is much smaller than pumped flow. Why is it still enough after a trip?`,['Decay heat is only a few percent of full power','The core stops making heat as soon as the rods drop','The pumps always restart automatically within a minute','It isn\'t enough on its own, so the core must be flooded'],0,`Removing a few percent of full power needs far less flow than full power.`)])
]});

U({id:'radiation',title:'Radiation protection',icon:'Sv',color:'#639922',desc:'Dose and its units, limits, time, distance and shielding, contamination and ALARP.',lessons:[
L('p1','Dose and its units',10,[
T(`<h3>Measuring dose</h3><ul><li><b>Absorbed dose</b>, in <b>grays</b> (Gy): energy absorbed per kg (1 Gy = 1 J/kg)</li><li><b>Equivalent dose</b>, in <b>sieverts</b> (Sv): absorbed dose × a weighting factor for the type of radiation</li><li><b>Effective dose</b>, also in Sv: allows for how sensitive each organ is. This is what dose limits use.</li></ul><table><tr><th>Radiation</th><th>Weighting factor</th></tr><tr><td>Gamma, X-rays, beta</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Neutrons</td><td>2.5 to 20 (depends on energy)</td></tr><tr><td>Alpha</td><td>20</td></tr></table>`),
N(`An organ absorbs 0.5 mGy of alpha radiation (weighting factor 20). What's the equivalent dose?`,10,0.01,'mSv',`0.5 × 20 = 10 mSv. Alpha is very damaging if it gets inside the body.`),
T(`<h3>UK numbers</h3><ul><li>Average UK dose from all sources: about <b>2.7 mSv per year</b>, about half from radon gas</li><li>Legal limit for classified radiation workers (IRR17): <b>20 mSv per year</b></li><li>Limit for members of the public from work activities: <b>1 mSv per year</b></li></ul><p>Most nuclear power station workers receive well under 1 mSv a year.</p>`),
W('doseCompare',{},`Click the bars to compare everyday doses with dose limits.`,null),
Q(`Which has the highest radiation weighting factor?`,['Gamma','Beta','Alpha','X-rays'],2,`Alpha particles deposit their energy very densely, so they get a weighting factor of 20.`)]),
L('p2','Time, distance and shielding',12,[
T(`<h3>Three ways to reduce dose</h3><div class="eq">dose = dose rate × time</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">dose</span><span class="km">total radiation dose received</span><span class="ku">sieverts (Sv), usually mSv or µSv</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">dose rate</span><span class="km">dose per hour</span><span class="ku">Sv per hour (e.g. µSv/h)</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">time</span><span class="km">how long you stay</span><span class="ku">hours (h)</span></div><div class="kn">Match the time units: µSv per <b>hour</b> × <b>hours</b>.</div></div><ul><li><b>Time</b>: spend less of it near the source</li><li><b>Distance</b>: for a small source, dose rate falls with the square of distance (the <b>inverse square law</b>)</li><li><b>Shielding</b>: each <b>half-value layer</b> (HVL) halves the dose rate. For cobalt-60 gamma rays, the HVL of lead is about 12 mm.</li></ul>`),
N(`The dose rate is 400 µSv/h at 1 m from a small source. What is it at 4 m?`,25,0.01,'µSv/h',`4 times the distance → 4² = 16 times less: 400 ÷ 16 = 25 µSv/h.`),
N(`The HVL of lead is 12 mm. Behind 36 mm of lead, what fraction of the dose rate is left?`,0.125,0.01,'',`36 mm is 3 HVLs: ½ × ½ × ½ = 0.125.`),
W('jobPlanner',{},`A 30-minute valve repair is near a source giving <b>2 mSv/h at 1 m</b>. Use distance and lead shielding to keep the job's dose <b>below 0.1 mSv</b>.`,`Combining distance and shielding multiplies their effects. Radiation protection staff plan jobs like this before work starts.`),
Q(`You need to cut your dose from a job in half quickly. Which is usually easiest?`,['Double your distance from the source (4× less dose rate)','Work twice as fast, even if it means rushing the job','Stand half as far away but finish the job quicker','Take off your dosimeter so it records less dose'],0,`Distance is often the cheapest and most effective protection. Rushing causes mistakes; removing a dosimeter breaks the rules.`)]),
L('p3','Contamination and ALARP',8,[
T(`<h3>Contamination vs irradiation</h3><p><b>Irradiation</b> is being exposed to radiation from a source. <b>Contamination</b> is radioactive material in the wrong place: on skin, clothes, tools or surfaces. Contamination can spread, and can be breathed in or swallowed, where alpha and beta emitters do much more harm.</p><p>Radiation areas are classified as <b>controlled</b> or <b>supervised</b> areas, with protective clothing, personal dosimeters and contamination monitors at exits.</p>`),
Q(`A worker's glove shows contamination at the exit monitor. What's the main concern?`,['The glove now gives out radiation that can harm anyone who looks at it','Contamination could spread or be swallowed or breathed in','The glove will heat up and catch fire from the radioactivity','There\'s no concern: contamination on a glove can\'t go anywhere'],1,`That's why contaminated items stay in the controlled area, and radiation protection staff are called.`),
O(`Put the steps for leaving a controlled area in order.`,['Finish the job and leave the work area','Remove protective clothing at the boundary','Check yourself at the contamination monitor','Leave if clean, or call radiation protection if not'],`Monitoring after removing protective clothing makes sure nothing leaves on your skin or own clothes.`),
T(`<h3>ALARP</h3><p>UK law requires doses to be kept <b>as low as reasonably practicable</b> (ALARP), not just below the limits. Every job with radiation exposure is planned to reduce dose wherever it's reasonable to do so.</p>`),
Q(`Your electronic dosimeter alarms on a high dose rate. What should you do?`,['Ignore it and finish the job quickly, then report it afterwards','Stop, leave the area by the way you came, and tell radiation protection','Turn it off and on again in case it\'s a false alarm','Move closer to find the source so you can tell them where it is'],1,`Treat alarms as real until proven otherwise. Stop, withdraw to a lower dose area and report.`)])
]});

U({id:'plant',title:'Plant systems',icon:'PWR',color:'#5F7A8C',desc:'A PWR tour, the pressuriser, steam and electrical systems, instruments, safety systems, materials and chemistry.',lessons:[
L('s1','A tour of a PWR',10,[
T(`<h3>Three circuits</h3><ul><li><b>Primary circuit</b>: water at 155 bar is pumped through the core and the steam generators. It's radioactive.</li><li><b>Secondary circuit</b>: water boils in the steam generators, drives the turbine and is condensed. It's kept separate from the primary water.</li><li><b>Cooling water</b>: sea water (at UK coastal sites) cools the condenser.</li></ul><p>Hinkley Point C's EPR reactors each have four primary loops, each with a steam generator and a reactor coolant pump, plus one pressuriser.</p>`),
W('pwrDiagram',{},`Click the components to learn about them. Then press <b>Quiz me</b> and find each one.`,`Every system you'll learn is somewhere on this diagram.`),
Q(`Is the steam going to the turbine in a PWR normally radioactive?`,['Yes: the water boils in the core and goes straight to the turbine','No: the steam generators keep primary and secondary water separate','Only at night, when the turbine runs on stored steam from the core','Only at full power, when some primary water mixes into the steam'],1,`That separation is a key PWR feature. Steam generator tube leaks are monitored closely.`)]),
L('s2','The pressuriser',12,[
T(`<h3>Holding the pressure</h3><p>The pressuriser is a tall vessel connected to a hot leg. It's partly full of water and partly full of steam, at saturation.</p><ul><li><b>Heaters</b> boil more water, raising the pressure</li><li><b>Spray</b> of cooler cold-leg water condenses steam, lowering the pressure</li><li><b>Relief and safety valves</b> protect against overpressure</li></ul><p>When the primary water heats up and expands, it surges <b>into</b> the pressuriser and compresses the steam, so pressure rises. When it cools, it surges <b>out</b>, and pressure falls.</p>`),
W('pressuriser',{},`A power change is about to cause surges into and out of the pressuriser. Use the heaters and spray to keep pressure between <b>153 and 157 bar</b> for 40 seconds.`,`In a real plant this is done automatically, but operators must understand it and be ready to take over.`),
Q(`Pressure is too high. Which action brings it down?`,['Turn on the heaters','Open the spray','Add boron','Raise turbine load'],1,`Spray condenses steam in the pressuriser, lowering pressure.`),
Q(`At Three Mile Island, the pressuriser relief valve stuck open, but the panel showed it closed. What did the light really show?`,['The valve\'s real position, from a sensor on the valve','That the valve had been told to close','That the pressure had dropped back to normal','That the pipe after the valve had cooled down'],1,`The indication showed the command signal, not the valve's real position. Plants now have direct position indication, and operators are trained to check other symptoms such as temperatures downstream of the valve.`)]),
L('s3','Steam generators, turbine and generator',10,[
T(`<h3>Steam generators</h3><p>Hot primary water flows through thousands of U-shaped tubes. Secondary water boils around the outside of the tubes. Operators control steam generator <b>water level</b> with feedwater flow: too low and you lose the heat sink, too high and water can be carried into the turbine.</p>`),
Q(`Steam demand suddenly increases. What does steam generator level do first?`,['Falls straight away, then recovers','Rises at first ("swell"), then falls','Stays the same, then slowly rises','Falls, then rises above normal'],1,`Steam pressure drops, so bubbles in the water expand and the level rises for a while: swell. The opposite, shrink, happens when demand drops. This makes level control tricky.`),
T(`<h3>Turbine and generator</h3><p>Steam expands through high- and low-pressure turbine stages, spinning the generator. The UK grid runs at <b>50 Hz</b>, so the generator must spin at an exact speed:</p><div class="eq">rpm = 120 × f ÷ number of poles</div><div class="key"><div class="kt">What each part means</div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">rpm</span><span class="km">revolutions per minute of the turbine and generator</span><span class="ku">per minute</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">f</span><span class="km">grid frequency</span><span class="ku">hertz (Hz) = cycles per second; 50 Hz in the UK</span></div><div class="krow"><span class="ks">poles</span><span class="km">number of magnetic poles in the generator</span><span class="ku">no unit</span></div></div>`),
N(`Hinkley Point C's turbines use 4-pole generators on the 50 Hz grid. What speed do they turn at?`,1500,0.001,'rpm',`120 × 50 ÷ 4 = 1500 rpm.`),
Q(`If the turbine suddenly trips at full power, where does the reactor's heat go?`,['Nowhere: the heat simply builds up in the core until the pumps stop it','Steam dump valves send steam straight to the condenser, and the reactor is tripped or quickly reduced in power','Into the grid, which absorbs the extra energy as electricity for a few minutes until the turbine is restarted','It stays in the turbine, which keeps spinning freely until the steam runs out'],1,`Steam dumps (turbine bypass) and relief valves protect the primary circuit from overheating while power is reduced.`)]),
L('s4','Nuclear instrumentation',10,[
T(`<h3>Three overlapping ranges</h3><p>Detectors outside the reactor vessel measure leaking neutrons. No single detector covers all 11 decades, so there are three ranges:</p><table><tr><th>Range</th><th>Typical detector</th><th>Measures</th></tr><tr><td>Source range</td><td>BF<sub>3</sub> proportional counter or fission chamber</td><td>individual pulses (counts per second)</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate range</td><td>compensated ion chamber</td><td>current (amps)</td></tr><tr><td>Power range</td><td>uncompensated ion chamber</td><td>% of full power</td></tr></table>`),
W('niRanges',{},`Move the power level and see which instruments are on scale. Find a power level where the <b>source and intermediate ranges overlap</b>.`,`During a startup, operators confirm overlap before the source range is switched off. There must never be a gap in indication.`),
Q(`Why is the intermediate range ion chamber "compensated"?`,['To make it read faster, so it can keep up with the quick power changes that happen during a startup at high power','To cancel out the current caused by gamma rays, which would swamp the small neutron signal at low power','To make it cheaper, because it needs less gas than an uncompensated chamber','To protect it from the heat of the core, which would make its readings drift high'],1,`At low power, gamma rays from fission products can produce more current than neutrons. A second chamber, sensitive only to gammas, cancels them out.`),
Q(`Why are source range detectors switched off at higher power?`,['To save electricity, because they\'re only useful during refuelling','They would be overwhelmed and could be damaged by the high count rate','They are only needed when the turbine is offline and the grid is quiet','At high power they switch over to measuring the coolant temperature'],1,`Once the intermediate range is reliably on scale, the source range high voltage is switched off. It's switched back on during shutdown.`)]),
L('s5','Safety systems and defence in depth',12,[
T(`<h3>Barriers</h3><p>Radioactive material is kept in by several barriers, one inside another.</p>`),
O(`Put the barriers in order, from the inside out.`,['Ceramic fuel pellet','Fuel cladding','Reactor coolant pressure boundary','Containment building'],`Each barrier backs up the others. Defence in depth also includes good design, operations, automatic protection and emergency planning.`),
T(`<h3>Reactor protection system</h3><p>The protection system trips the reactor automatically on dangerous conditions: high power, high startup rate, low or high pressure, low coolant flow, low steam generator level, high temperature and more.</p><p>It's built to be <b>redundant</b> (several channels), <b>diverse</b> (different ways of detecting a problem) and <b>fail-safe</b> (loss of power causes a trip). Channels often vote <b>2 out of 4</b>.</p>`),
W('voting',{},`Each channel trips when it sees a problem. Using 2-out-of-4 logic, cause a reactor trip with the <b>minimum</b> number of tripped channels.`,`Two channels are needed. One faulty channel can't trip the reactor on its own (no spurious trips), and one failed channel can't stop a real trip.`),
T(`<h3>Emergency core cooling</h3><p>If coolant is lost, emergency core cooling systems inject borated water: high-pressure pumps, pressurised accumulators, and low-pressure pumps for long-term recirculation. Hinkley Point C's EPRs have <b>four independent safety trains</b>, each able to cool the core on its own, plus a core catcher and a double-walled containment.</p>`),
Q(`Why use 2-out-of-4 voting rather than 1-out-of-1?`,['It\'s cheaper, because it needs fewer cables than one channel','It avoids trips from a single faulty channel and still trips if one channel fails','It\'s faster, because four channels share the work and react to a real fault sooner than one','Regulations ban single channels, but they work just as well'],1,`It balances safety (no single failure can stop a trip) and reliability (no single fault causes a trip).`)]),
L('s6','Materials and chemistry',10,[
T(`<h3>Embrittlement</h3><p>Steel becomes brittle when cold. Years of neutron bombardment raise the temperature below which the reactor vessel steel behaves in a brittle way. So:</p><ul><li><b>Pressure–temperature limits</b>: when the vessel is cold, pressure must be kept low</li><li><b>Heat-up and cool-down rates</b> are limited (typically about 55 °C per hour) to limit thermal stress</li><li><b>Pressurised thermal shock</b>, cold water hitting a hot vessel at high pressure, must be avoided</li></ul>`),
Q(`Why must pressure be kept low when the reactor vessel is cold?`,['Cold water is heavier, so it presses harder on the bottom of the vessel','Cold steel is more brittle, so high stress could cause a fracture','The pumps won\'t work at high pressure until the water has warmed up','Boron comes out of solution in cold water and can block the pipes'],1,`Pressure–temperature limit curves come from this. Operators must stay inside them during heat-up and cool-down.`),
T(`<h3>Water chemistry</h3><ul><li><b>Boric acid</b> controls reactivity</li><li><b>Lithium hydroxide</b> balances its acidity to reduce corrosion</li><li><b>Dissolved hydrogen</b> removes oxygen made when radiation splits water molecules</li><li>Corrosion products activated in the core, like <b>cobalt-60</b>, cause most of the dose to workers during outages</li></ul>`),
Q(`Why is hydrogen added to PWR primary coolant?`,['To make the coolant lighter, so it is easier to pump','To remove oxygen produced by radiation, reducing corrosion','To slow the neutrons down, so more fission happens','To raise the pressure, so the water doesn\'t boil'],1,`Radiation splits water into hydrogen and oxygen. Excess hydrogen pushes the reaction back, keeping oxygen very low.`),
N(`A heat-up rate limit is 55 °C per hour. What's the fastest you could heat up from 60 °C to 290 °C?`,4.18,0.02,'hours',`230 °C ÷ 55 °C per hour = 4.2 hours.`)])
]});

U({id:'operator',title:'The operator',icon:'✓',color:'#534AB7',desc:'Human performance tools, lessons from accidents, and a startup from start to finish.',lessons:[
L('o1','Human performance tools',12,[
T(`<h3>How control room teams avoid mistakes</h3><p>Operators work in shift teams and use proven tools every day:</p><ul><li><b>STAR</b>: Stop, Think, Act, Review, before any action</li><li><b>Three-way communication</b> for instructions</li><li><b>Peer checks</b> and <b>independent verification</b></li><li><b>Procedure use and adherence</b>: follow the procedure; if it doesn't match the plant, stop</li><li><b>Questioning attitude</b>: if something seems wrong, it probably is</li><li><b>Conservative decision-making</b>: when unsure, put the plant in a safe, stable state</li></ul>`),
Q(`What does STAR stand for?`,['Start, Turn, Adjust, Reset','Stop, Think, Act, Review','Safety, Training, Action, Report','Steady, Trip, Alarm, Restart'],1,`It takes a couple of seconds and prevents a lot of slips.`),
O(`Put three-way communication in order.`,['The sender gives the instruction','The receiver repeats it back','The sender confirms the repeat-back is correct','The receiver carries out the action'],`This catches mishearing before it becomes a mistake.`),
W('phonetic',{},`Control rooms use the phonetic alphabet so letters can't be misheard ("bank D" vs "bank B"). Type the word for each letter. Get <b>5 in a row</b>.`,`Valve and pump names often include letters, so this comes up constantly.`),
Q(`While raising power, you see an indication you can't explain. What do you do?`,['Carry on to the target power, then investigate once things are steady','Stop, place the plant in a stable condition, and investigate with your supervisor','Ignore it if the other indications look normal, and note it in the log','Raise power faster, so you reach a stable state sooner and can check then'],1,`Conservative decision-making: never continue into the unknown.`),
Q(`A procedure step doesn't match what the plant is doing. What should you do?`,['Work out a fix yourself, then carry on with the steps','Skip that step and carry on with the next one','Stop, keep the plant safe, and tell your supervisor','Repeat the step until the plant does what it says'],2,`Procedures are only safe when they match the plant. A mismatch means something unexpected is happening.`)]),
L('o2','Lessons from accidents',14,[
T(`<h3>Three Mile Island, 1979</h3><p>A feedwater problem tripped the reactor. A pressuriser relief valve opened, then <b>stuck open</b>, but the control panel suggested it had closed. Coolant escaped. Pressuriser level looked high (steam was forming elsewhere), so operators <b>reduced emergency cooling</b>. About half the core melted. Very little radioactivity escaped and nobody was harmed, but it changed the industry.</p><p><b>Lessons</b>: better control room design and indications, symptom-based procedures, much more simulator training, and sharing of operating experience.</p>`),
Q(`Why did the TMI operators reduce emergency cooling?`,['They wanted to save the emergency water in the tanks in case it was needed later on','Pressuriser level looked high, so they thought the system was too full of water','The emergency pumps had failed, so there was no water to send','They were ordered to by the grid, to keep the turbine running'],1,`They trusted one misleading indication. Operators are now trained to check several independent indications and think about what the plant is really doing.`),
T(`<h3>Chernobyl, 1986</h3><p>During a test at low power, reactor 4's RBMK core was in an unstable state: xenon-poisoned, with far too few control rods inserted, and with a <b>positive void coefficient</b>. When the operators pressed the shutdown button, the rods' graphite tips briefly <b>added</b> reactivity at the bottom of the core. Power surged to prompt critical, and steam explosions destroyed the reactor.</p><p><b>Lessons</b>: inherent safety in design (negative feedback), never operating outside the safety limits, and the idea of <b>safety culture</b>, which came out of the investigations.</p>`),
Q(`Which design feature made Chernobyl's RBMK so dangerous at low power?`,['A negative void coefficient','A positive void coefficient','Too much boron','Steam generators'],1,`More steam meant more reactivity, a positive feedback loop. Western PWRs have negative void and temperature coefficients.`),
T(`<h3>Fukushima Daiichi, 2011</h3><p>A huge earthquake struck. The operating reactors shut down automatically, but off-site power was lost. Then a tsunami flooded the emergency diesel generators and electrical switchgear: a <b>station blackout</b>. With no way to remove <b>decay heat</b>, three cores melted, and hydrogen explosions damaged the buildings.</p><p><b>Lessons</b>: protect against extreme external hazards, keep backup power and mobile equipment ready, and plan for long-term decay heat removal.</p>`),
Q(`What do all three accidents have in common?`,['Prompt criticality, caused by operators pulling the control rods out too quickly while trying to raise the power back up','Losing control of heat removal or reactivity because the plant\'s real state wasn\'t understood or protected against','Sabotage of the safety systems by people who wanted to damage the plant','Faulty fuel from the factory that broke apart inside the reactor core'],1,`Each one led to big changes in design, training and regulation, and they're studied by every operator in training.`)]),
L('o3','A reactor startup, step by step',12,[
T(`<h3>Bringing it all together</h3><p>A startup uses nearly everything in this course: reactivity, 1/M plots, startup rate, nuclear instruments, feedback, xenon and boron. Before starting, the team calculates an <b>estimated critical position (ECP)</b>: where the rods should be when the reactor goes critical, allowing for boron, xenon, samarium and temperature.</p>`),
O(`Put the main steps of a reactor startup in order.`,['Calculate the estimated critical position and check shutdown margin','Withdraw the shutdown banks','Withdraw the control banks in steps, plotting 1/M','Reach criticality with a startup rate below about 1 DPM','Confirm source range and intermediate range overlap','Raise power to the point of adding heat','Connect the generator to the grid and raise load'],`Then power is raised in stages, with boron adjusted to keep the rods where they should be.`),
N(`At the last startup, the reactor went critical at 60% rod withdrawal. Today, xenon adds −200 pcm and nothing else has changed. The rods are worth about 10 pcm per % near there. What's the estimated critical position?`,80,0.01,'% withdrawn',`You need an extra +200 pcm from the rods: 200 ÷ 10 = 20% further, so about 80%.`),
Q(`During the approach to criticality, the count rate rises much faster than your 1/M plot predicted. What do you do?`,['Keep withdrawing, but more slowly, and watch the plot','Stop withdrawing, insert rods if needed, and reassess','Turn off the source range, as it must be faulty','Carry on: 1/M plots are never accurate near critical'],1,`Never continue past an unexpected response. Something isn't as you thought.`),
Q(`What is the "point of adding heat"?`,['The point where the turbine starts to turn and the generator joins the grid','The power level where fission heat is enough to raise temperatures, so temperature feedback starts to act','The point where the pressuriser heaters switch on to raise the pressure','The point where the reactor reaches 100% power, so the temperatures stop rising and the feedback switches off'],1,`Below it, power can rise without any feedback. Above it, the negative coefficients start to take effect, and the startup rate falls off by itself.`),
T(`<h3>You're ready for the control room</h3><p>Open the <b>Control room</b> tab. Mission 1 is a full approach to criticality using a 1/M plot, on a simulator built on the same physics you've learned. Good luck, operator.</p>`)])
]});

